[2/200] Running: Explain the architecture of a transformer model....

🧠 LLM Response:


The transformer at its core is an encoder decoder model which is considered as a subpart of sequence-to-sequence models. You can train this model to perform the same task as recurrent models, but use less resources in the process.

An encoder is designed as a layer of modules for performing certain tasks.

Imagine that the input consists of only a single word. The target word has no context and it can be replaced with any other word from the vocabulary. This should end with a word taking the place of another word.

Now, in case the input consists of multiple words. Here, context plays an important role. nearby words also affect selection of the output word. There needs to be a mechanism that allows information transfer between words.

For that reason, the output from an encoder is augmented with additional information. There is nothing like recurrent connection here. The model in the encoder is stacked, processing input words in parallel and handling interference between words in a different way.

Encoder:
1. Input Embedding
2. Positional Encoding
3. Token & Position embedding
4. Masked Multi-head Self Attention
5. Dense Layer
6. Residual Connection and Layer Norm
7. Feed-Forward Layer
8. Residual connection
9. Layer Norm

Decoder:

Masked Multi-head self attention on decoder output.

Entire output word by word
