## 1. No attention

Before attention models were introduced, translation models had all other parts - input encoders, output decoders and the cross attention between input and output sequences. Only attention was missing. Output at time \(t\) was solely dependent on output at time \(t - 1\). It never attended to the input sequence.

In the figure below, you can see model architecture of an encoder-decoder model without attention.

LSTM encodes the input sentence into a vector - encoded sentence. Fully connected layer (dense layer) gives the probabilities of start tokens for translated sentence. Another LSTM continues building the translated sentence but it attends only on the previous hidden state but not on the input sentence.

When the entire sentence is encoded into a single vector, the following problems may appear.

- Every word is encoded into a fixed-length vector. Such vector lacks a capacity to faithfully represent a word.
- Sentence length is limited! Google Translate limits sentences to 60 words. If in addition the sentence uses long words, it may happen that the translated sentence is incomplete.
- Modeled translation probability is factorized over time.
- Alignment between source and target words is arbitrary. The figure below shows an example of source-to-target alignment. Why it is arbitrary? When it comes to convolution models (ConvS2S), there is a structure that allows high-level concept information (expressed in a lower-resolution input) to be used in parallel with low-level concept prevision (expressed in a higher-resolution input), to compute the output sub-phrase. This might be why the example shows how words are discontinuously aligned.

For variable length source and target sequence, we should consider the distribution \(P(Y, \mathbf{y}_j | X)\) where \(X\) is a source sentence, \(Y\) is a target sentence and \(\mathbf{y}_j\) a \(j\)-th word or phrase in a target sentence \(Y\). Then ask yourself the question - for which part of input sentence \(X\) is \(j\)-th target word or phrase generated? This part is the focus of attention which helps us to build a better translation model.

Inclusion of a single input sequence and partial output regards machine translation as a sequential modeling task. Indeed it is a sequential modeling but the dependency between input and output, as well as between the elements of the output sequence, is not fully taken into account. Adding attention remedies this problem.

This approach is resiliant to long sentences and complex morphologized or agglutinative languages such as Turkish, Hungarian, Finnish, Basque, Estonian and Japanese.

## 2. Langauge models

It is well known that a language model, such as OpenAI GPT-3, can multiply improve state-of-the-art results for many down-stream tasks. Take as an example language modeling with extremely long context and multi-task objective and how it improves performance on many NLP tasks.

To encourage accurate modeling of dependencies between sequence elements, instead of factorizing entire sequence into individual elements \(P(X)\), attention model factorizes sequence into a product of conditional probabilities.

\[ P(X) = \prod_{i = 1}^{n}{P(\mathbf{x}_i | \mathbf{x}_{i - K}, \cdots, \mathbf{x}_{i - 1})} \]

where

- \(X\) is the entire input sequence
- \(P(X)\) is the probability of input sequence
- \(\mathbf{x}_i\) that is an input at sequence position \(i\)
- \(n\) is the number of sequence elements
- \(K\) is the number of previous sequence elements that we use to predict the next one.

This may explain some confusion why introducing conditional probabilities to arrange sequential factors into a graph-like structure. When these dependencies are represented with nodes, we end up with a graphical model.

Here’s the catch. When you think of \(X\) as an source sequence, it turns out that
the factorization equation above expresses two types of dependencies (probabilities):

- \(P(X)\) - dependence of input sequence on itself
- \(P(\mathbf{y}_t | X)\) - dependence of output sequence on the input sequence

