## Gradient

Gradient at a point is the first order derivative (it’s a matrix) of a function at that point. But when we say gradient while optimizing a function, we actually mean the rate of change of a function at a specific point.

## Gradient Descent

Gradient Descent is a optimization algorithm. Based on the gradient, it updates the variable to minimize or maximize a differentiable function. One of the main issues with gradient descent it that the convergence is sensitive to learning rate.

To understand why it’s sensitive, let’s understand what’s happening under the hood. Gradient descent is a discrete version of following differential equation

$dx/dt = - \eta \nabla L(x(t))$

Here, L is the loss and x is variable and $\eta$ is the learning rate. Here is what the left hand side trying to tell us regarding the behavior. If we stretch t, we can think it as discrete steps(the gif below is equivalent to t).

Here is the discrete version

$x(n+1) = x(n) - \eta \nabla L(x(n))$

As we are doing a discrete update, our small step in continuous domain might result in a larger step in the discrete domain. Additionally, the derivative might be changing quickly which might make matters worse. These problems can be controlled by choosing a lower learning rate but it will take time to reach minimum or maximum(depends on what you choose). There are some adaptive learning rate methods available such as adagrad(just one name among few names), which is based on the hessian(The second derivative of function), to deal with these problems.

## Vanishing Gradient Problem

Vanishing gradient problem can be understood with this simple model! I call it a toy model. :D Vanishing gradient problem is associated with neural net or more specifically; deep neural net. Let’s not take side tracks! Now check this post for the basic understanding of neural network model.

A simple neural net with two input units, one output units and one hidden layer(no bias units) has following parameters:

1. W1 , W2, W3 , weights of input to hidden layer
2. W4 , W5 , W6 , weights of hidden layer to output layer

