Now, check that mixed term in denominator. Don’t get afraid, let’s see why I mentioned it. Let’s compare it with activation function tanh(.)

As, we can see, tanh(.) is not going to change its behavior a lot for a small change in x, thus the derivative is not going to be a large number(it is around 1 for 0). Now, all terms in the denominator are bounded by one.

In python, exp(x) > 1e4 for x >= 9 . Now, if the total sum of incoming variable to hidden activation function is large then the gradient is really small which will lead to a vanishing gradient!

The same phenomenon happens for large activation function or biased activation function as sigmoid(.). Check this for understanding different activation function.

As a result, gradient is not able to change weights of the nets which lead to partial ordering or change in false parameters and leaves the true parameters out. This can lead to no change or convergence to someplace which might not be a minimum.

