## 1. Introduction

To train models beyond the capacity of a single GPU, it is necessary to scale across multiple GPUs. Parallelism can be classified into two categories: data parallelism and model parallelism. Unfortunately, the model parallelism part is often neglected. In this post we discuss some basics of model parallelism on the basis of Megatron-LM and then introduce GShard, a model parallelism library.

Since this post also serves as a reference to DeepSpeed and GShard, a little familiarity with the tech report of Megatron-LM and a short survey by OpenAI is recommended. This post:

1. Clarifies the common questions regarding model parallelism
2. Discusses sophistication of pipeline model parallelism and the new feature of FSDP in PyTorch
3. Briefly introduces GShard as the end-to-end solution to model parallelism

Maximizing GPU use and efficient model parallelism enable faster experiment turnaround. Techniques like gradient checkpointing allow for designing large models and training them on expensive datasets. As a bonus, TL;DR formula to compute sequential time taken in both model and data parallel approaches is presented at the end.

## 2. Basic Model Parallelism

The motivation behind data and model parallelism is that when the model becomes large, the memory on the particular GPU will fill up. To learn more about data parallelism, refer to the code samples from the tutorial. PyTorch apparently provides a distributed parallel wrapper through DDP.

Model parallelism aims to divide the model across multiple GPUs. Of course, what works out of the box with model parallelism is to load a part of the model manually onto each GPU. But ultimately, what we want is that massive multi-GPU model training should be easy. This requires automatic model partitioning.

## 3. Through Pipeline Model Parallelism and Megatron-LM

Of course, data parallelism is not sufficient to scale up large models. To do this, we will dive deeper into model parallelism and parallelize the forward and backward computation of a model. The most important Github library for this task is Megatron-LM. Megatron-LM, while a reference for several projects, does not suit our needs completely. Here we want to understand small details regarding model parallelism that Megatron-LM addresses:

1. Hybrid model parallelism is proposed. It proposes taking a big and complex module that does not fit on one GPU, and using model parallelism within the module while using data parallelism to train the entire model.
2. Model parallel region of the modules in Megatron-LM relies on intra-layer model parallelism. To do this, partition the inputs to the layer along the model parallel dimension and create (model parallel) replicas (you will see that the module is divided horizontally). The replicas have the same weights so that the same computation is performed, but with different portions of the input. Then all the outputs will be collected into a single tensor. (Remember that data parallelism is about replicating everything and using collective operations.)
3. Megatron-LM uses a special kind of weight initialization (activation response normalization) to deal with the layer normalization problem when using higher level of parallelism. The new module will now do scaling for you, with RMS calculated as `sqrt(hidden_size / parallel_size)`.
4. One thing we overlook while talking about model parallelism is that the order of embedding lookup is different than data parallel. This is because the model parallelism is done in each input, and then it’s divided across GPUs. But if we want to chat over a manufacturer’s telegram and find out the next token, in that case the parameters will be different. This can be interpreted as assuming that RNN (or any model, perhaps different models) is trained to generate each word independently. Although all parallel models can be considered as training under communication delay, the use of different parameters is like providing an additional layer of description to the sequence.

Megatron-LM can automatically learn when to shard (partition) and when not to shard. However, it assumes homogeneous consideration (i.e. when there are fixed layers, we cannot do model parallelism for only a sub-selection of the layers). Megatron-LM also supports Tensor Parallel Model Parallelism as a substitution to Pipeline Parallelism.

## 4. Multi-node Model Parallelism with GShard

In practice
