GPT: Generative Pre-trained Transformer is a language model developed by OpenAI that has been trained on a large corpus of textual data to generate coherent and contextually relevant responses to a given input text. It belongs to the family of auto-regressive language models, where the model is trained to predict the next word in a sequence of text based on the preceding context.GPT-2: This is a successor to GPT, with a larger model size and more parameters. It has been trained on a much larger corpus of text and is capable of generating coherent paragraphs of text when given a prompt.BERT: Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer is a language model developed by Google that has been trained on a large corpus of textual data to understand the semantic meaning of words and sentences in a given context. It belongs to the family of bidirectional language models, where the model is trained to predict a missing word in a sentence based on the surrounding context.BERT is capable of understanding the contextual meaning of words based on their usage in a sentence, and is widely used in downstream applications such as text classification, question answering, and sentiment analysis.

The main difference between GPT and BERT architectures is that GPT is an auto-regressive language model, where the model is trained to generate text based on the preceding context, while BERT is a bidirectional language model, where the model is trained to understand the semantic meaning of words and sentences based on the surrounding context.Additionally, BERT incorporates a bidirectional attention mechanism that allows it to better understand the meaning of words in a contextualized manner, which allows it to perform more complex tasks such as sentiment analysis and question answering, while GPT is generally better suited for text generation and summarization tasks.
[12/200] Running: How does prompt engineering affect language model outputs?...

🧠 LLM Response:


-Nisha Talagala

Recent advances in AI safety have generated much interest in the behavior of language models.  For instance, Theano models have been studied for their tendency to generate unsafe replies with only asterisks (or other symbols) for all the letters in their words.  Prompt engineering is a technique that has been used to get around this limitation.  Prompts, or changes to the original text of an utterance or statement, are techniques for injecting thoughts or questions into the generated language model to generate a different sentence that still has the same meaning.  This generates completely new texts that are attributable to the original speaker rather than a machine.

Prompt engineering is a relatively new language model, but I think it’s already on the mainstream in some areas.   For instance, in legal text, the lawyer must choose his or her words carefully. It’s a bit like playing a game of connect the dots, the more the dot is off the game center, and the harder it is to win.  As the party voting is ultimately the client, it is in their best interest to give him or her the text they actually want to read. By inserting the prompt, you can give the user the words they need to figure out what it means.

For this post, I’ve focused on describing the technical aspects of prompt engineering.   The core idea is that you generate a response and send that response back through a loop of other alterations and replies to keep the user engaged. An example of this is when I asked people for clarification on something.  That way, if you want to talk about something more than a few words, you can ask more than one person.  Of course, if you’re giving feedback to someone, that means that they can’t just answer the same question multiple times.  Also, if you include extra comments or steps to help keep the conversation engaging, it’s much easier to make sure the user is getting the desired response.

Generating the text also gives you a good chance of getting a clean and fully-formatted text for various reasons: selecting author names and subject lines, generating the language model text in a different format (e.g., HTML), reducing the length of the generated text, or just making sure that the output is more consistent.

In addition, generating a text and sending it back to the user gives you a chance to improve the sentence structure in the generated text.  You can do this by including words that don’t quite fit, by changing the order of statements, etc.  These are all examples of tweaking the composition to increase the length and frequency of the original statement.  The important thing is that you read and re-read the generated text several times to make sure the essential components are all there.   I’ve found this is especially useful in political text.

In short, prompt engineering allows you to play around with ideas that might never have occurred to someone using natural language.  I must admit, however, that I haven’t tried to use prompts much in legal text, as I find the prompt tends to be quite narrowly focused on specific topics. So, next time you’re drafting your next few sentences, stop and reflect on the impact that generating the text will have on your goals.

