It is a standard result in machine learning theory^{2} that \[I_{\beta,\beta} = {\operatorname E}_{\beta}( d\log p_\beta(w)/d\beta)^2.\]

In the paper^{1}, the authors argue that the matrix \(E\) has another interpretation via language model learning. Namely, the \(i\)-th row is a vector that represents the second word of the bi-gram ‘\(i\) <space> ‘.

Just like \(\hat{Z}_k\), we have that \(E_i\) is a vector in \({\mathbb R}^L\). Now, lets introduce the normalised representation \(e_i\) of these vectors in the cache-key space as \[e_i = E_i/\|E_i\|_2.\] These unit vectors live on a unit sphere embedded in \({\mathbb R}^L\). The paper 1 argues that as we train a transformer model, the set of vectors \(e_i\) start to look something like this:

Now suppose we have a cache that contains bi-grams of the form ‘\(i\) <space> \(w\)’. A naive approach for cache-retrieval would be to look-up bi-grams using exact matching. This would run us square into the curse of dimensionality. Indeed, we would have to store \(O(K(LN)^2)\) items in the cache, where \(K\) is the size of the vocabulary, and \(LN\) is the width of the embeddings.

We can reduce this memory cost dramatically using approximate nearest neighbour searching [3]. The accuracy of the nearest neighbour retrieval depends on how well-clustered the vectors on the latent sphere are.

If the vectors are well clustered, then we can do nearest neighbour searching by simply storing only the cluster centroids. We can store these vectors as \({\mathbb R}^L\) vectors in the cache. Now the question becomes how we decide the clusters. It turns out that language models find different clusters at different times. When we are processing the word at position \(k\), the language model bias \[\operatorname{head}(\hat Z_k/ \|\hat Z_k\|_2),\] acts as a query point for the ANN search over the cache-vectors. We can think of this query point as the centroid of the cluster that the model is focused on at time \(k\). We still do nearest neighbour searching but now we only retrieve items stored in the cluster centred on the query point.

Of course, this explanation is just a metaphor. But I think it’s a pretty good metaphor that explains not only what the layer normalization does, but how to choose the width parameters and how to do dimensionality reduction.

In particular, lets recall that we can embed a humanly comprehensible vocabulary into \({\mathbb R}^{10000}\). We can also explain how to do embed sub-word information into higher dimensional spaces without any loss of data [5]. This also explains why its hopeless trying to cluster longer phrases.


