### What is quantization exactly?

Quantization reduces the precision of a neural network's tensors and operations from floating point to fixed point numbers. The lower precision introduced hundreds of times during inference processing cycles add up to significant performance benefits.

Here's a visualization of how full precision (a.k.a.) 32-bit float interacts with data for the neural network.

We can see how the full precision numbers overlap with the histogram at all levels of the data. Now look at post-training quantization, which quantiizes weights and activaitons without re-training the model:

Notice how the upper and lower quantization steps snap into the lower bound of the histogram based on just the "min" and "max" of the dataset. As a result, post-training quantization introduces some accuracy loss, although there is significant performance improvement in processing time.

Why is approximating the network values so close to the data min and max important? Because people expect and depend on the diagnostics their neural networks produce, so there should be as little accuracy loss as possible with quantization. To get around this issue, people started quantization-aware training (QAT) which quantizes inference graphs and retrains the model. Afterwards, the network re-converges around the accuracy loss from quantization:

This quantization scheme uses fake quantization with backpropagation with both sensitivity or divisions getting involved in tensors and activation calculations. In general, QAT uses approximations to address huge accuracy losses introduced by post-training quantization.

Dynamic quantization is a way to dynamically round weights on the fly as they come in real-time from the sensor. Because rounding weights for the mean of the whole dataset tends to distort the high and low end of the data, we use dynamic quantization to determine the quantization bound from the statistics of the current mini-batch itself. Even though dynamic or flying quantization is incredibly expensive, it allows for dynamic and temporary quantization for every inference tick.

Adaptive quantization is surprisingly similar to dynamic quantization, except it allows us to re-quantize the network as per the current mini-batch being processed by the neural network. It is less costly than dynamic quantization but more costly than post-training quantization.

Quantization can be applied in either int8 or int16 patterns. The quantization formula below shows how a floating point value gets reformed into a fused float to integervalue and back to float or dequantized floating point:

### When to use quantization?

Using quantization simulation on integer-based hardware such as CPUs or MCUs prove to be extremely efficient as shown in the graph above. However, even GPUs and more specialized hardware architectures, quantization proves to be useful too:

Accuracy comparisons for the two major quantization-aware training models - TensorFlow Lite and PyTorch - show that both have roughly the same performance and accuracy loss of static QAT.

Well, what happens if we used a different quantization policy for fine and coarse quantization for layers? Depending on your accuracy or performance goals, you could choose either for the "quantization aware training" outputs.

Saving the model post quantization will also allows you to run it on real hardware or run it through an emulator on your laptop, which is notoriously much faster.

### So quantization is great for my latency and performance requirements, but how much computing overhead will I have to pay?

