Neural networks use distributed representations, where several tasks interact with each other. How can we visualize what a neural network captures? Here we illustrate how to fine-tune a model for a particular task and visualize the attention layers related to this task.

## Train a Language Representation model on a generic corpus

Here we use the Tensor2Tensor implementation of a Transformer. Here we follow the specifications described in the paper “Using the Explicit mapping to reduce hallucinations”.

Here also we use Wikitext-103 as a generic corpus for training “Transformer language Model”. Data used (original data is here http://einstein.ai/data/wikipedia) for training is obtained by running the following dialog on Tensor2tensor:

```
cd $T2T_PATH
eval "python3 tensor2tensor/bin/t2t-datagen --problem=wikitext_103 --data_dir=$DATAPATH --tmp_dir=$TMPDIR
```

This creates two files in data_dir: train.tfrecord and valid.tfrecord. We train a model on train.tfrecord using:

```
cd $T2T_PATH
python3 tensor2tensor/bin/t2t-trainer --t2t_usr_dir=$T2T_PATH \
 --data_dir=$DATAPATH --problems=translate_from_english --model=transformer \
--hparams_set=default_transformer_lm_2021 \
--output_dir=$MODELPATH --train_steps=1000000
```

## Selecting a layer of interest

Attention heads weight the importance of certain tokens. All heads combined construct a representation of the sentence. We can visualize what a certain head pays attention to across different layers by taking a sentence like this:

Picture by Sahin Ege (Stock.xchng)

Here the first token `< s >` refers to the beginning of sentence token, the last token `[{EOS}]` refers to End of Sentence. The hidden states of a Transformer layer are the result of the self-attention heads and feedforward layers. For more information see the Tensor2Tensor transformer specification. The number of attention heads is defined in the `hidden_size` argument of the transformer. Here we use a Transformer with `hidden_size = (8 * 8) = 64`.

In the plot above each row represents the network prediction at a certain layer. When a model is trained, we save the weights regularly in one “checkpoint”. So we can also analyze checkpoints and visualize what happens when a new checkpoint is loaded. It is recommended to open a terminal per tab or to put the above graphs in subprocesses, so the graphs can respond quickly.

## Visualizing Attention layers per head

Each attention head attends a certain part of the network. Some attention heads attend to the beginning of a sentence, some attention heads attend to the end, and some will attend all over the place.

The above code takes the predictions of allhead_weights to produce three visualizations:

The first is a color code for each attention head. The heatmap represents, what a certain head pays attention to. Here blue pixels show strong attention weights whereas red pixels show low attention weights.

Then the second visualization shows the most attended tokens per head. For every attention head, we collect the highest weights and draw bar plots.

Lastly, we visualize the variance of each attention head over the different attention heads. The variance is between `0` and `1`. Heads with a lot of variance are very different from other heads. So a high value suggests, that a certain attention head has an important function. Heads with smaller variation are shown by darker colors.

