Perplexity is a measure of how unsure (or confused) a model is when generating a prediction during translation. The units of perplexity are computed as powers of 2, where the higher the number of bits (2 to a larger exponent), the more perplexed your model is. Now how is this perplexity computed? We are encoding all the sentences in our corpus with fixed-length binary codes. We then take the average number of bits needed to encode a word in the corpus. The formula is the power of two to this value.

Perplexity in machine translation...

The most straightforward metric you can use isPerplexity Perplexity is defined as the exponentiation of the entropy, which can be interpreted as the (geometric) average branching factor of a language (the number of “equally most probable” next words). Lower perplexity means that your model is more confident when it makes predictions. This metric doesn’t necessarily correlate with evaluation metrics like BLEU. So keep in mind evaluating language models for translation tasks is different.

