 What happens if a sentence contains words that aren’t in the vocabulary.

### Overview

I will answer the following questions related to the paper BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding:

1. Describe the function of the tokenizer’s vocabulary. What happens if a sentance contains words that aren’t in the vocabulary.
2. Describe the role of positional embeddings (including why they are used) BERT understands input as n sentences transformed into a continuous representation (vectors), so the model cannot understand the order of the word in certain position which is misleading. The positional embeddings encode per-position information in a sequence in order to make prediction related to ordering of a sentence. The paper (Absolute positional embeddings have been proved useful in various sequence modeling tasks, mainly those involving recurrent neural networks, but its use with attention-based architectures and token embeddings haven not been widely studied.) ^{1}
Describe how BERT uses the Masked Language Modelling (MLM) method to train a bidirectional representation.

### Masked Language Model

Mask Language Model is one of the training tasks that BERT performs so, How does it works?

MLM objective is randomly picking some of the tokens in a sentence and predicting them given the rest of the sentence. The process contains the following steps:

- Randomly select certain percentage of words in sentence
- Replace them with a `[MASK]` token
- Concatenate the result to give input to the model
- The output of the model are set of classifications that represents prediction of the original token of the `[MASK]` token.
The risk that the model might overfit to the `[MASK]` token could be minimized during training by the way described by the authors of the paper in the next section abstract and the following cool gif describing the whole process. ^{2}

### Abstract

> We compare a number of pre-training objectives for language modeling and find that bidirectional models sometimes learn better representations compared to left-to-right or right-to-left models. Unlike previous work, we mask the input randomly, rather than always from the end, and run extensive experiments to determine the optimal percentage of tokens to mask.
>
> To prevent the model from overfitting to the token margins, we predict randomly masked tokens, instead of following the left-to-right computed masking process. About 15% of randomly selected tokens are masked, and the embeddings that represent the mask tokens ([MASK]) are not used at other places. Instead, the actual input tokens are fed to the the next word prediction objective.

### Masked or Permuted Language Models

In section 1.3 Randomized Masking , The authors of the paper has mentioned several points about the motivation for the random masking over using standard masking, such as:

First: Previous work on language models was based on standard sequential masking which is better suited for auto regenerative language models that used for natural language generation and their problematics such as over fit models.

Second: Using the first word as a position for prediction can put the model to predict a recognizable token in most of the cases (e.g. “I, We and others”), so the pre-training data becomes not significant and makes the pre-training reduce.

So In our view, a pre-training objective should meet three criteria: process the whole sentence without dividing it into independent tokens, use the context on both directions, and keep the original vocabulary so the model does not have to learn the relationship between a token and a position.

What this implementation do differently from the original BERT paper?

Several strengths and weakness were addressed in the original paper as following.

> Evaluation of performance at the token level like traditional language modeling metric is higher for unidirectional models. The reason is that evaluating the likelihood of tokens requires a distribution only over the vocabulary, which favors models such as PET that were trained using a language modeling objective. This measure also can not evaluate the bidirectional models, since words and their context usually co-occur , which unidirectional models can exploit, but the bidirectional models can only exploit this under certain constraints (like casual language model) or must to be evaluated and modified to fit the constrain (like permuted language models). When we evalated scores at the sentence level and evaluated the perplexity according to the language model per word. All the bidirectional models (Masked and Permuted) are better.

[31/200] Running: What is causal masking in transformer decoders?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How to implement it in my network? What effect does it have on the decoder output? Those were exactly the questions I had in mind when I was faced with the task of implementing a transformer decoder. I was relatively new to the networks based on attention mechanism and those questions made me dive into the very basics of actions inside a transformer, from the input embeddings up to the final decoded sequence. In this page I will not only attempt to clarify what is causal masking and why we should do it, but I will also try to show how that magical layer of causal masking can easily be implemented on your own. Hopefully, this might save you some time trying to understand decoders in general and also make implementing such networks easier than relying on libraries. Most of the things covered here are for all Masked Attention networks, not just Transformers and/or Decoders.

### For starters, what is masking and why do we need it?

Before we move on, let’s first say a few words about the transformer architecture, as we will be focusing on the decoder’s part:

- The decoders take as input sequence a sequence of one-hot encodings of tokens (words)and return the sequence that we need to generate, token by token. I would suggest you to always think of the encodings as vectors of one-hot actions, because sometimes thinking of them as tokens to be decoded can complicate the picture.
- The encoders take as target the same as the “sequence that we need to generate” from the decoder and return a sequence of vectors usually called “value”.

Now, looking at the decoders part only, we can distinguish two attention levels – the masked multi-head attention, which takes as input word embeddings of the input sequence of desired length, and the multi-head attention on the encoder’s output. We need masking in the decoder’s first level uniquely for the auto-regressive task – in order to predict the next token based on the already decoded previous ones. I will not go into any details for the second level of attention here, so let us continue with the first masked multi-head attention.

Can you guess from the name what is the purpose of this particular attention level? Self-Attention! Self-attention simply means that we will be calculating attention scores of how strong the connection is between any two input words in the source sentence that we are trying to reconstruct. But, you may say, if this is not the encoder’s part, how come we are “reconstructing” and not “generating” a sequence?

At training time, the decoder is not aware of the reconstruction-generation-whatever we might call it. It is presented with label-target pairs – having the answer to the question makes it good at learning how to look for it. At inference time of course, we can finally make use of the encoding vectors produced from the encoder and use the decoder part to teach it to generate unknown resulting answers corresponding to the source input sentence.

The masking part of the masked multi-head attention layer is only done at training time, so we can teach the model a property called auto-regressive prediction. By auto-regressive prediction we mean exactly what it means – regressing on auto values. In other words, if I am at position i, I can see everything to the left from it. And what does left-of-i mean?! That’s an additional trick. It means that the decoder has seen the answer and we simply mask the sequence in such a way so that the decoder pays attention to the answer vector up to the current position, meaning the word at position i. And, there you go, you get a self-attention mechanism just right there!

But fear no more, in a few moments, you will figure out how to implement it – it turns out it is, simply put, just zeroing some matrix elements.

Now, let’s look at what do we have: we have a sequence of tokens and we are using initial position embeddings to represent them. Their value may be pre-trained, but often they’re just randomly initialized. So, they keep no information about the word. I like to assume that they are trainable, as it is easy to imagine what you want them to learn. Then, we have a self-attention mechanism. It works in some magical way, but why do we need it? Why don’t we just pass the initial embeddings to the encoder-decoder attention and train the network to output the desired sequence?
