[33/200] Running: Explain the difference between encoder-decoder and decoder-o...

🧠 LLM Response:


Grönqvist et al. (2020) suggests that language models can be seen as decoders trained with the objective of predicting the next word, where the next word is represented as an embedding. Solving this next word prediction lends itself to many downstream tasks, but certainly it is not trained to do answer questions, sentiment, or translation directly, etc. as it is often used in open source practice.  The encoder-decoder method uses encoders to encode some knowledge, which may include the actual item to be translated or answered, along with other contextual information in addition to this, and this knowledge, which has been encoded, gives the decoder more information to give an answer.

Are generative models more powerful than discriminative models?

Another way to think about the language models is to think of them as generative models on arbitrary contexts. OpenAI’s GPT-3 and Google’s T5’s paper offer new state of the art on answer a wide variety of domains: stock manipulation, arithmetic, sentiment analysis, translation, etc. with little to no parameterization. A particularly interesting element, is in a “decoder-only” model, any input to the model is context info, which it produces some output (for example something like the probability you got sentiment, the probability of a translation, etc.), so in other words, all contextual info the decoder-only model is fed gives the decoder-only model the opportunity to ’empty the memory contents’ of its learned information depending on what the question is.

Different methods of implementation seem to drive no-code ML performance. @Dakuo ‘s paper “Transferability of Data Features in Classification: An Empirical Study” (active on arxiv as of Aug 12, 2021) finds feature transferability as a factor for a model’s predictive ‘out of domain-performance’. B. Melnyk‘s paper “Analysis of Attribution Transferability and Adversarial Subspace Interpolation in Neural Networks” finds most deep neural networks (DNN) architectures perform roughly the same with a few (GoogLeNet) performing better compared to the others on transferability.

More updates and analysis, here.
[34/200] Running: What is zero-shot vs few-shot vs fine-tuned inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


## Zero-shot inference#

Zero-shot inference uses a model’s latent understanding of a general task. This means you can use the model to perform a general task without needing to fine-tune for a specific task.

Zero-shot inference is generally used for generation (e.g. text-generation, image generation) and binary classification, and is supported by such models as Instruct, GPT2, XLNet. Zero-shot generation aims to create text that looks like text in the training dataset, whilst zero-shot classification will give you the probabilities of two labels to classify a binary class.

### Advantages#

Using zero-shot methods there is no need to curate a fine-tuning dataset for each task.

### Drawbacks#

It is possible sometimes to get unpredictable results as depending on your application without being able to instruct the model’s dataset more finely it could confuse different tasks.

Some tools such as FLAN are built on top of other models and use fine-tuned zero-shot strategies.

## Few-shot inference#

Few-shot inference fine-tunes models with up to 100 examples in the pre-training stage. Using few-shot methods, an understanding of the task can be gained from the pre-training examples, then easily applied to the new data.

### Advantages#

This method can gain a basic understanding of the task and apply it to new data in a quick, scalable way, without the need to commit to large data and compute resources for fine-tuning.

### Drawbacks#

Some problems will have a higher effectivity if the model is fine-tuned to the task, and few-shot inference does not utilize the full potential of the model as fine-tuning small batches.

## Fine-tuned inference#

Fine-tuning is the process of training a pre-trained model on a large task-specific dataset. This method presents the model with more data and task-specific examples so that the model can adapt to the particular task and provide a more accurate prediction.

### Advantages#

Increases model performance and accuracy as the model is trained on a larger amount of task-specific data than current state-of-the-art models are in a similar domain.

### Drawbacks#

Fine-tuning involves curating a suitable dataset for the task that the model is being trained on, and inference requires a lot of resources, such as GPU memory.

There are other methods that are particularly suited to certain tasks such as, prompt-tuning, p-tuning.
