[40/200] Running: What is distillation in model compression?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Distillation is a machine learning technique that works on the principle of transferring information from a complex neural network to a smaller, and simpler neural network, so that, the smaller network performs to the best of its limitation. The main advantage of this method is that you can deploy simpler models to production without compromising the performance and the smaller networks can function in low resources, such as our phones or edge devices, so our applications can perform with similar performance as big neural networks.

Let’s try to understand what distillation is with an example of a teacher teaching a student about linear algebra concepts.

Figure 1. Distillation process for simpler models

The teacher – has studied linear algebra for like 5-6 years and is completely master of it – the student – is a newbiew who just heard about algebra and is very very excited to learn

Without distillation – The teacher would spend much time transferring a lot of information and overloading the student with a lot of knowledge, which makes the student difficult to comprehend and stress. Possibly the student’s grades are good but he does does not fully enjoy the process.

With distillation – The teacher spends time simplifying the concepts and teaching the simplified concepts gradually and gradually challenging the student towards high level concepts. The student very well understands the concepts and although the student has a limited time to study, his performance is good.

There have been multiple papers trying to achieve the human level learning. I would recommend you to read Hinton’s paper on Distilling the knowledge in a neural network [4] as it established the concept of knowledge distillation in model. Come, let’s train some models.

## 1. Dataset

Can we use the same teacher and student dataset. It’s recommended no. But you can. In some cases, It is beneficial to use the same dataset for teacher and student. But as we said earlier, teacher being the master, it’s best to load some extra information to make its performance much better than the student. So we will use different datasets for teacher and student in this experiment.

Teacher: Transfer learning from imagenet tensorflow dataset
Student: Pets dataset from Oxford

Both the datasets have similar kind of data but coming from different distributions. For example, Imagine we have a flow of images, some of the images are both from pets dataset and imagenet dataset. But then there is a perturbation from imagenet dataset towards unusual images which helps the teacher to generalize better on the unusual type of images coming to the student for testing.

## 2. Network configuration and details

Teacher: Resnet-101
Student: MobileNet

Why resnet-101? Because it has 101 layers between input and output layer and they have residual connections where multiple convolutional layers hear the output directly from the input, similar to out direct 360 hearing of all in that classroom.

Why mobilenet?
For mobile application and devices which are puny resource constrained devices! Just in case you don’t know about mobilenet, check the mobilenet [2] paper and train for image classification [3] as it has made so much noise for inference and no noise on training part. It will change your perception on gigantic neural networks.

Fun fact: I think someone was selling t-shirts with mobilenet logo.

