[45/200] Running: How does TTFT relate to user-perceived latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:


photo credit: Slack’s Blog

## Time To First Tweet explained

According to Slack’s Blog in Jan 2019, in addition to user satisfaction surveys, the following metrics also measure your user’s perception:

- MTTS – The Mean Time To Success (MTTS) is the time it takes to load the Thread List, having fetched the required data successfully.
- TTFT- Slack defines TTFT as a metric that is measured as “time from when the window loads until the full text of one Tweet is visible for the user in their feed.”

Expressing their success, they see TTFT as a very straightforward metric. If a user can see a tweet, then they can interact with it — at this point, the user has value! Thence, the shorter the TTFT, the faster the onboarding of the service, the happier the user.

Now, what was Twitter’s problem, and how was it fixed?

The feed view relies on dozens of cached API calls to fetch the data it requires to paint the UI. The initial load included 50+ unique requests to their API backend, sorted into several categories.

Wanting a system efficient enough so as not to slow the page load but powerful enough to support users with 10k+ tweets, Twitter jettisoned redundant or less important API calls to increase the speed.

Following that, Twitter improved metrics for payload sizes as a signal for cost, using API instrumentation (server-side), user-facing instrumentation (client-side), and a payload viewer using the Transport Inspector.

Finally, as additional tips for improving the TFTT and decreasing the payload size, Twitter offered a few interesting strategies:

- The elimination of all non-essential calls.
- Recursively requesting more data from the server only when users highlight or request more.
- Deferring of non-essential work through timeouts, and making it fire and forget.
- Consolidating API calls so that multiple requests are sent and merged in the backend.
- Eager fetching of some data and caching smaller subsets.

You can read the full report here, and learn more about optimizations to user-recognized metrics, and additional methods such as caching and running code on fewer threads, to improve Twitter user performance.
