Viewing transformer as a black-box ensemble of sub-networks.

Transformer is popular because of its simplicity and expressive power in natural language processing. It can also be used in any sequence to sequence prediction problem. But what truly lies inside the transformer?

We will focus mainly on transformer encoders. In a previous blog post, Stacked Autoencoder is not Special, I illustrated autoencoders can be split into encoders and decoders. Conceptually, transformer is an autoencoder. In fact, from byte-level language modeling, transformers can be generalized to RNN structures. Replacing transformer layers with LSTM or other recurrent layers are therefore valid.

Attention maps tell very little about what a black-box model does internally. Each transformer decoder block can be viewed as a black box sub-network. The network consists of internal sublayers at deeper levels (the various arrows, in blue). An added positional embeddings to the input vector is transformed by the decoder sub-network, into some source embeddings.

Most technical articles out there describe attention mechanisms in terms of dot product between query ${Q}^{T}$ and key ${K}^{T}$ (symbol ${T}$ denotes transpose). This view is deprived of explanatory power, as to what’s truly going on in the linear transform $W$, $U$, $V$ and the nonlinear function.

Suppose our decoder blocks processes on a single vector of shape $d$, If $m$ is the size of $W$, column-stacking $W$ into $dim(m)=(d,m)$ and $dim(U)=(m,d)$ is an equivalent matrix representation of the operations carried out by $W$ and $U$. The gist of this linear algebra representation (like the mnemonic for Hebbian rule from neuroscience), is that W project the input vector $x$ onto $m$ dimensions. The linear transformation $U$ then projects the vector back onto the original $d$ dimension. The nonlinear activation converts the resulting scalar into a ${R}^{+}$ range. This details seems to have been omitted from most technical articles and discussions out there on the internet.

Why are such transformations necessary?

Consider a simple case, where $d=2$. Suppose we are working with word embeddings (doc2vec), with a two dimensional vector projected onto the plane. For example, $[1,2]$, $[-2,1]$, etc. We can think of W as a n-dimensional projection. If we set the weights to all ones, the weights $m$ controls how “receptive” each projected subspaces in W is to the original input $x$. In terms of “sample space,” we can think of this operation to enlarge the space of representation, allowing information to flow through more efficiently.

Note how reshaping the $1 \times d \times d$ weights into $d \times d \times m$. The dimensions of $UWx$, where $dim(W)=(d,m)$, $dim(U)=(m,d)$ then becomes $(d,d)$, after reshaping and transposing.

Stacking makes for large matrix multiplication. The input $x$ is a linear combination of input vectors $x={x}_{1},...,{x}_{n}$. It is essential to batch the inputs, since in recurrent models the computation scales quadratically with the number of sequence steps.

It is important to pay attention to the shapes of weights $W$, $U$ and $V$. Because only when $V$ forms a linear combination with $m$, $W$ and $U$, does attention scores $Attn$ remains in $1\times d$ dimensions. For skip connections to work, TrmW and TrmU must have the same shapes as the original input vectors $x$.

Last, it is the linear transform V that transforms the attention scores to the model’s hidden states, and onto the residual connection.

For example, if attention is an identity function ([1,1,1] becomes [1,1,1]), we see from the code that TrmV$dim(V)=(d,d)$. Weighing the attention weights and masking out the relevant values, the TrmV matrix, with shape (d,d), then projects the attention scores $xWU$ back into a $1\times d\times 1$ shape.

