# Introduction

The T5 model is large but popular. However, due to limited GPU memory, it is hard to fit a model onto a single GPU. Possible solutions include to reduce the batch size or the number of layers, but both would degrade the model quality. Large batch sizes enhance the efficiency of BERT's training but also harm model convergence. To combat this problem, timm-unet 2.0 provides two efficient solutions, focusing on handling the large batch size and memory issues.

We recommend that you use two Clusters described in [Guide on How to Set up](./guide-on-how-to-set-up-environment.md). The detailed requirements are defined below.

**Note**: The node `worker0` is considered as the master node hereafter.

# Distributed Tensorflow based training

By default, TF2 is used. For compatibility reasons, use `tensorflow==2.10.0` or greater version, an older version is used. Check out [distritbuted tensorflow based training](https://test.graiax.cn/xenith/Timmy/welcome-to-doc/speech/generation/t5/) for more detailed examples.

For training parallelism, you can use `tf_cluster_resolver.TFConfigClusterResolver` to replace `MultiWorkerMirroredStrategy`. `--cluster_file` should be set accordingly.

This example is to use four L4 gpus. Each GPU utilizes a warmup and stepwise decay learning rate schedule. Notably, the learning rate will be twice that of a single training run due to the distributed training setup.

Tensorflow parallel distribution

```shell
python train_dist.py \
    --root=timm_unet2-guide \
    --model=timm_unet2 \
    --dataset=ImageNet \
    --vocab_size=21841 \
    --num_layers=12 \
    --layer_depth=12 \
    --repeats=12 \
    --head_size=64 \
    --dropout=0.2 \
    --output_dim=768 \
    --ffn=32 \
    --include_position_embedding \
    --mfu_length=196 \
    --min_loss_scale=1e-5 \
    --quant_per_channel \
    --traindir=~/datasets/imagenet/plitted/train
