🧠 LLM Response:
 Is there any difference between text and audio generation? How can such challenges be overcome?

# Token-by-token text generation

In text generation, it is often easier to take a model trained on next token prediction, and use the model for autoregressive generation. The model then outputs tokens one-by-one in a greedy manner. The model is often of the form \(P_\theta(w_t\vert w_{t-k},\dots,w_{t-1})\), i.e. can condition on a limited window of the preceding \(k\) tokens.

In order to use neural network models for generation, the trick is to use teacher forcing. During training the predictions (the generated tokens) may be compared with the actual forward-looking tokens. At test time, the generated tokens are fed into the network following standard autoregressive generation.

While this works well, it may run into the problem of generating non-fluent output (where the output is too ambiguious to be meaningful), and fails to accomplish long-term coherence.

The Ambiguity Problem: The default solution is to sample only from the unigram distribution over categories and greedily choose a category at every token position. This may be bad for fluency because not all token-combinations will be gramatically correct, or semantically consistent in the output. To overcome this, we may need to look back more than one token, to consider joint distributions but this scales very slowly.

The Coherence Problem: In describing a scene, the model must use tokens that adequately describe the scene without redundantly repeating the same information. For long-term coherence, we must have some way to refer to entities already mentioned (e.g. “He” refers to the pet). We may include an attention-mechanism over the previous tokens.

The Attention-Mechanism models dependencies between hidden states, using a soft-selection over the history of past tokens and maintains locally important tokens.

The Encoder-Decoder Network plays an important role in maintaining coherence by using two distinct subnetworks. The first subnetwork takes an input sentence and embeds it into a low-dimensional representation, while the second subnetwork takes a low-dimensional representation and outputs a feasible sentence. This architecture can be used for document summarization, where the input sentence may be long and the objective is to create a more concise representation of the same input. Such a network may also be used for machine translation between two different languages.

# Challenges in audio generation

While text can be classified into distinct tokens and languages follow a universal grammar, music cannot be classified as easily. Usually, exact notes or quantized notes are sampled from discrete contours. In other words, there are choices for what to sample.

Although, it is possible to develop a Vocoder that extracts a discrete latent representation Z from the audio signal A. After training the Variational Autoencoder \(P(Z\vert A)\), we can set up the loss function between the actual signal and the extracted signal. However, these functions are parameterized differently and need gradients to be set up.

This problem has been explored in Connectionist Temporal Classification (CTC) Loss, for ASR.
