This talk examines the ethical considerations related to generative models, focusing on how they can be misused for deception and malicious purposes such as Deepfake facial reenactment. It explains the challenges involved in storing generative models, including the counterproductive measures taken by researchers to suppress them, the ease of replicating models by anyone with the necessary resources, and the decision to retain the models indefinitely to allow for faster iterative model design. It highlights the growing importance of understanding the ethics of generative models and the need for education in this area.

- Tech Lead, Generative Models
Julian Togelius

Julian Togelius is a Tech Lead, Generative Models and part of the GrandAI initiative at Hugging Face, an AI researcher and associate professor at NYU Tandon School of Engineering. He is associate professor of computer science and engineering at New York University Tandon School of Engineering, and co-director of the NYU Game Innovation Lab. He received his M.Sc. in Computer Science (2004) and his Ph.D. in Information Technology (2007) from the IT University of Copenhagen.

Togelius works on a wide range of topics related to computational intelligence in games and game design. His long-term goal is to make AI a widely used technology in and around games, making games both cheaper and more fun to develop and play. He is also a prolific game jammer, and in fact won the game jam event Ludum Dare 20, earlier known as the world’s largest game jam. He is also the editor of the book “Procedural Content Generation in Games“.

Current research topics include the following:

– General video game playing
– Player modeling
– Interactive PCG
– Computational complexity of PCG
– Content generation for VR/AR
– PCG via machine learning
– Computational creativity in games
– Procedural personas
– Games and AI education
