 Despite the universal success of these models, we generally don’t have a precise answer to this question. In this work, we examine transformer language models of varying sizes and architectures on four different massively multilingual datasets. We find that, despite best practices, the cross-lingual performance of these models does not improve as we train larger models on more multilingual data. To probe the inner workings of these models, we develop new techniques for extracting significant subnetworks from multilingual models based on both gradient (𝜔-S score) and behavior analysis (synaptic saliency; 𝜅-S score). These methods identify small subnetworks of the models that contain the multilingual knowlege. We show via delete-and-randomize experiments that these subnetworks are both necessary and sufficient for solving multilingual tasks.
