[56/200] Running: Explain how masked self-attention works in BERT....

🧠 LLM Response:
 What limitation does it address? How is masking performed?

BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) is a natural language processing (NLP) model developed by Google in 2018. It is based on the transformer architecture and is designed to understand the context of words in a sentence by considering the words that come before and after it.

Masked self-attention is a technique used in BERT to capture the dependencies between words in a sentence. It addresses the limitation of the vanilla transformer architecture, which only considers the words to the left of the current word in each layer of the network. In masked self-attention, the attention mechanism is modified such that each word can only attend to the words that come before it. This allows BERT to capture the dependencies between words more accurately, as it takes into account the context from both directions.

Masking is performed by adding a special “[MASK]” token to the input sequence at the positions of the words that are to be masked. The model is then trained to predict the original word at these positions, thus making it better at understanding the context of the words in the sentence.

In summary, masked self-attention is a technique used in BERT to capture the dependencies between words in a sentence more accurately by only allowing each word to attend to the words that come before it. This addresses the limitation of the vanilla transformer architecture and allows BERT to better understand the context of words in a sentence.

Masked-Multi-Head-Attention

