Transformers are uniquely suited to Neural Machine Translation, which is a sequence-to-sequence problem (unlike image classification, which is a scalar-to-scalar or vector-to-scalar problem).

Encoders convert each word (or token) to an embedding vector (a vector composed of 0s and 1s) and require the mask for self-attention (because both the query and key vectors are obtained from a common sequence).

Basically, choosing the “right” mask is one of the most important decisions you make about your API. The decoder feeds the embedding and the output of the previous step as inputs into self-attention, which is then connected to the encoder via a multi-step attention process.

Transformers provide the flexibility to combine both contiguous and non-contiguous chunks of data sources. For example, consider the encoder-decoder architecture shown in the figure below. (Figure from the article Measuring the Effect of Information Bottlenecks in Neural Machine Translation (PDF).) Token levels can now be combined using single or multiple heads. In the example below, there are two separate transformer heads, each with its own unique query vector to calculate attention values.

When we train the transformer to translate, we usually apply an autoregressive token in the supervision step. Autoregressive means that, in the case of the encoder, the next token enters the model as input, while the decoder produces the predicted token. More precisely, the decoder is trained to predict the next token given the input token and remaining output tokens. When it has learned this function, it can produce one token after another in the inference to generate entire sequences.

