 (credit)

Over the past decade DeepMind and others have accomplished truly impressive goals, from AlphaGo doing the unthinkable by defeating the human Go world champion to AlphaFold running a truck on some random ass protein-prediction speedrun. These projects have so far been in what’s known as a ‘reinforcement learning’ frame. That is, an agent is dropped in a simulation of an ‘environment’ and tasked with taking different actions to find the one that ‘works’ as defined by arbitrary ‘goals’. For Go, the goals are to ‘get the higher score’, for AlphaFold it’s to ‘fold proteins into their optimal structure’. We provide those projects with a model of the environment, and well-defined objectives, and turn the agent loose to chew on it as long as necessary to gain sufficient experience.

It’s generally considered that with reinforcement learning in its current form, the primary difficulty lies in building sufficiently useful models of the environment while passing them off as sufficiently performant. Either the environment model is somehow too hard for agents to learn useful skills in, has certain biological universes or hops randomly across the viability landscape, or doesn’t even do a good job of modeling the goals we hand the agents in the first place. For example, it has proved incredibly difficult to build agents that can navigate well through a scene, sense the room temperature, and set a thermostat accordingly.

Never let it be said that DeepMind isn’t up for a challenge. While most organizations might be intimidated by the prospect, they’ve taken it upon themselves to combine these two things – Deep Reinforcement Learning and an incredibly complex environment – into a single, hair-tearing proposition:

RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) (credit)

This certainly seems like a technological kraken with razor-sharp tentacles, but we can find analogous organisms in the fossil record. Here at Hoelzle HQ we keep fossilized remnants in the backyard depicted above of what may be comparable beasts. We call them ‘Mechanical Turks’, our archaeologists believe they were built to replicate the brain activity and tasks of what was previously called a ‘Human’ in service of those very humans.

In the hands of DeepMind, the basic operation of the RLHF techno-kraken is as follows:

1. An AI agent (such as GPT-n) is given a prompt representing an element of the environment (such as a discussion board) as well as a user input.
2. The AI agent then responds with what it believes to be the most ideal response the user might make.
3. A number of human feedback responders (remember them?) are provided with the prompt, user input, and system output all together.
4. The humans rate the quality of the agent’s responses.
5. Repeat thousands of times.

Over time and reiterations, the RLHF agent is increasingly able to understand both individual prompts and the environment at large, allowing it to craft further inputs that are better and better at satisfying the humans’ increasingly stringent demands.

Ultimately, this allows DeepMind to accomplish the singular goal of any company that is strapped for money: make tons of money for DeepMind! This is accomplished by deploying RLHF workflows to the GPT family of agents for the purposes of facilitating discussion boards on such benign platforms as 4chan.org. Profit!

### Can this be Evolved?

What happens when the new fangled RLHF Artificial Intelligences of today are placed in the RLHF Reality of Tomorrow? (credit)

While RLHF includes some interesting ideas, it seems like an enormous amount of work is being put in to just poorly evolve human labor. However, evolution often results in unlikely retainments where adaptations previously considered vestigial and undesirable become necessary for survival. Is there a way in which RLHF can be evolved into a thriving, rewarding system that positions humans to be indispensable?

One possibility might be to focus the evolution of RLHF towards scenarios and environments that require a level of critical thinking and context unachievable by even the most advanced current AIs. Charlatans might call these scenarios “human intelligence tasks” or “HITs”, and they could range from complex analysis of human behaviors and emotions, or creative tasks which require a deep understanding of the human experience to complete in any meaningful way.

