[59/200] Running: What is SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning) in LLM training?...

🧠 LLM Response:


SFT (supervised fine tuning) stands for Supervised Finetuning Simples, fine-tuning refers to the process of training an LLM on input-output pairs. SFT involves training the model in a supervised learning scenario. The model is presented with a set of input and output pairs and till adapt to learn the relationship between the input and the output. The goal of SFT is to enable the model to generate outputs that is indistinguishable from human demonstration when it receives novel test inputs.

SFT can be used to further refine pre-trained model and improve its performance on specific tasks, such as text summarization or language translation. In the SFT process, the pre-trained model is first fine tuned using labeled data that is specific to the intended task. The labeled data typically includes a set of input and output pairs. The input can be a text sentence or paragraph, and the output can be a summary, translated or classification labels depending on the tasks. The model is then trained to learn the mapping from input to output using techniques such as gradient descent and back propagation.

One of the key benefits of SFT is that it allows the model to learn the peculiarities of the downstream task. For example, if we are training an LLM to summarize news articles, we can use SFT to fine-tune the model with a set of articles and their corresponding summaries. This helps the model learns to identify the most important information in the article, and produce a concise summary that captures the essence of the article.

SFT also helps to improve the models generalization capabilities. By finetuning the model on a diverse set of training data, the model learns to recognize patterns and structures that are common across different inputs and outputs. This allows the model to generalize better to new inputs and produce outputs that are more accurate and reliable.

Overall, SFT is a powerful techniques to improve the performance of LLM. It allows the model to learn the specific characteristics of the downstream task and generalize better to novel inputs. This makes SFT an essential step in the LLM training process.
