## What is HPU

This section explains the role of Habana Personality Units (HPUs) in Gaudi chips. Any new AI model or idea involving convolution, activation, padding, local response, clipping, normalization, or anything else can be written as a specification and implemented in software. The software will be translated to be executed on the HPU with the help of an assembler and compiler.

The function that has to be accelerated will be coded in software called as HPU API, and it is transferred to the host driver and then translated to the HPU code and loaded on the HPU-attached SRAM.

Each HPU implements such a function and is pipelined in such a way that successive layers of the NN will be optimized on the HPU with minimum latency.

The NN is built around a set of parallel stages that are pipelined so that the pipeline is operating at maximum available input/output bandwidth. When the final output is produced, the next set of inputs will arrive and so on.

Gaudi core – It is a hardware-optimized architecture for Edge AI applications, especially CNN. It can be used for NN optimization and acceleration.

The core is activated and unloaded into the F2SRAM 32 (per core) as an intentional DDR4 memory interface from the Gaudi core, and that’s the reason why a very fast Gaudi core to F2SRAM 32 interface and DDR.

HPU Combo – The core bus interface (as a 32-bit manager), VLIW execution units (as a 4-bit manager), all floating-point data (as a 4-bit manager), and SRAM (as a 16-bit manager) are combined to form a single HPU combo.

Internal interfaces in the HPU – The Gaudi core idea and the HPU combo have three HPU busses where the bus between the Gaudi core and Gaudi core to F2SRAM 32 remain the same, the Gaudi core is connected to the HPU, the HPU is connected to the F2SRAM32, and the F2SRAM 32 is connected to DDR.

HPU Combo connectivity – This part discusses the basic connection between the Gaudi core and VLIW. The Gaudi core is the programming interface for the end user to access the HPU, and it communicates with the VLIW to execute the HPU combo.

Next, the Gaudi core will communicate with the Gaudi host and execute it on the HPU, and it will communicate with the DDR4 interface.

As the host core and the HPU communicate in both directions, the host core can send all the relevant information to the HPU so that it can execute at maximum speed.

HPU Combo execution – The Gaudi core communicates with the DDR interface by sending the program on how to execute at maximum speed, and then they communicate with the F2SRAM32, and then they generate the data and execute on the VLIW.

HPU combo architecture – The below diagram illustrates the overall architecture.

## Detailed HPU Combo design and block diagram

This section expands the HPU design from the architecture to the functional blocks and how to combine these blocks to form a functional block diagram and then use them to build a fully optimized ANN.

The upper part of the HPU combo is the core (host) that is accessed by the end application. When the user selects a particular application, it will go to the host, and the stage 4 CPU will access all the relevant instructions, such as the FFT, or filtering them with a specific target processor.

The HPU combo has four pipeline stages: start the computation, release the computation, put the results, and take the results.

HPU BUS 0 : start the computation : This section explains the bus support which is being used by the HPU combo to execute the desired task and is available to its users.

The first stage of the pipelining stage is to receive the instruction 1 from the host and put it into SRAM 0, and then the HPU BUS 0  will take the instruction 1, translate it to the F2SRAM32 0, and send it to the DDR interface.

