[61/200] Running: What is a causal attention mask?...

🧠 LLM Response:

    - A mask read by an attention model between any two tokens in a sequence. A value of 1 indicates that the model is allowed to attend to a token and a value of 0 indicates that the model is not allowed to attend to a token. Example in the image below where the input is a lot of text, and for each word, the model can only attend to previous tokens. That's because the attention mask for that token is a 1 for tokens before, and 0 for tokens after. You can see this causal attention mask on the left.
    - Padding masks are also important concepts in this. Padding masks, unlike other elements of this list, don't approach machine learning specific problems but rather technical concerns. When using normal attention on a sequence of tokens, a mask is used so that the model doesn't attend to the padding token. We could in theory use zero in the mask to prevent attention to the padding token, but modern versions of PyTorch or other popular machine learning libraries don't know the difference between the padding tokens that shouldn't be attended to, and other real tokens that happen to have a value of zero. Because of that, it's important to use a negative infinity padding mask. The end goal of the masks of course is to normalize and eventually get a softmax output of attention probabilities that adds up to 1, and using negative infinity ensures that a padding token will have zero probability of being attended to by any real token. You can see this done for the left image above using negative infinity (in the example Torch.nn.float("-inf") tensor objects). As a brief aside, this can be done with a padding mask of negative infinity or with simply 0s and -1e9.
    - A full attention mask is almost self explanatory, but it's an attention mask that allows for any value to attend to any other value. The mask contained all ones, meaning that each token can attend to anything in the sequence.

The answer to the prompt? Causal is correct. The excerpt above was taken from my other post about attention masks here.

