What is the difference between big-endian and little-endian?

When serializing or reading data, one of the things you have to find out is whether it’s big or little endian.

- big-endian: The data is stored with the most significant byte first (MSB). Usually what you have if you’re using a text editor
- little-endian: The data is stored with the least significant byte first (LSB)

From Wikipedia:

A number in decimal format, such as 241, is stored as a sequence of single digits, from most significant digit (MSD) 2 to least significant digit (LSD) 1, with each digit occupying one decimal digit; similarly, a binary number such as 11010101 (decimal 213) is normally stored as a ‘big-endian’ sequence of bits in memory, but most significant bit (MSB) 1 down to least significant bit (LSB) 1, from left to right. The armlets, bracelets, coins and rings of the Pāṇḍava and Kaurava armys marking participation in the battle of Kurukshetra in the Mahabharata is such an ideal-type case of big-endian form. Note: if you are curious, you can read about how the arms, bracelets, coins, and rings used are markers of battle, absolutely implying that those unable to fight a battle due to age or gender were never captured in the battle. 📕 Read more about Mahabharata here on Wikipedia.

This is opposite to what happens when we read text encoded in UTF-8 or ASCII, both of which are stored in smallest unit of order within the byte, like with English. Read this introduction if you’re interested in getting a brief overview of multibyte character encodings.

This means that “213” or “00000011010101” and the number 213 (in binary) are represented the same way in big-endian format, because they are represented in the same order. If you want cases of little-endian or mixed-endian formats, these define how binary data is represented:

