[67/200] Running: Explain the impact of batch size on training and inference....

🧠 LLM Response:


Batch size matters. For inference, larger batches amortize the cost of loading data and performing it throughput is higher. However, it impacts the runtime per sample for model training and inference. Batch size allows parallel computation of matrices and multi-threading to speed up executions.

Resolving only one examples per iteration adds to the runtime. To allow efficient parallelization of graphical process units, the minibatch size is N > 1, enabling the algorithm to compute N gradients at once. As the model updates the gradient after each pass, it is recommended to make the training slower while decreasing the batch size. Higher batch size requires more VRAM and a lower batch size reaches the maximum number of training steps because of the data augmentations. Therefore, it is advisable to never set batch size = 1 in general (unless during fine-tuning/transfer learning).

With regards to the model architecture, the influence of batch size on the performance of the model is often overlooked. In order to ensure proper fit on data, the model optimizer must set hyperparameters accordingly. Model optimization and data transformations often run in tandem with each other to iterate through different data subsets, because of the mini batch size. The implications of the influence of batch size are clearest when taking ensemble learning into the mix.

In contrast to the naive averaging of results over different models, bagging is a method that determines which subset of the data to be used in which model to be trained – in a random fashion but following sampling with replacement. Usually this task is achieved via estimators in the form of decision trees. As we replace observations into the data, the batch size becomes larger than the original data size. Following ensemble learning, it is essential part of stacking. Stacking is simply taking the output of a previous model and using the same as input variables in a subsequent model. The initial layer is called the base layer whereas the final layer is the meta-models. This works better than using linear combinations of classifiers together.

In general, for classification tasks, the recommended batch size is usually less than 32. The effect of the batch size on the loss is more evident when using a larger amount of training data. It is shown in the following image.

This image shows how the loss decreased over training iterations (epochs) using different batch sizes.

As the batch size is tuned based on the hardware constraints, the learning rate has to suit that batch size. A larger model trained with even larger batches would potentially require smaller learning rates, which in turn would increase the tuning complexity and demand far more computing resources. There are theoretical arguments to justify the learning rate to be inversely proportional.

## Adapt the batch size in function of the available memory

It makes sense to imagine that the larger the batch size the more memory it requires. However, as shown above, both the learning rate and the batch size are related in an usually interrelated way. The rule of thumb for the number of items to put in a batch follows a simple heuristic. When the training stage runs out of memory, then keep halving the batch size until it fits. When tuning the hyper-parameters, start with a batch size of 32 and increase it as needed.

### Example batch size progression

Using an NVIDIA A40 would require roughly 48GB for a resolution of 224, 533×533, using the Adem and AlexNet models based on DNN. For smaller architectures such as AlexNet, a batch size of 512 and 1027 for DLA-34 and ADEME would cause a fatal gpu-resource-exhausted-error. However both sizes would run without much difficulty for a batch size of 256. The fine-tuning stage requires decreasing the batch size even further.
