🧠 LLM Response:
 I approached with these questions but got lost in the sea of different deep learning frameworks each one touting its superiority over the others, each having its own community, and with the face of AI/ML constantly changing, it is becoming hard to keep track of all these frameworks.

Recently I stumbled upon the JAX library which is gaining quite a bit of popularity, especially in the fields of AI and machine learning. This sparked curiosity in me and I started looking at it in detail. This blog post is aimed to snatch you (just like me) away from their comfort zone and throw you into the lion's den of JAX by learning it the fastest way possible, that is working live with it through download code snippets and accompanying visualizations to analyze its internals. Also at the same time, learn the core concepts of machine learning like gradient, backpropagation, automatic differentiation, and more.

Let us start our journey from where deep learning is heading today and understand what are the new advancements that JAX brings to the table that could potentially leave the others (read NumPy, PyTorch, Tensorflow, etc.) dead in the near future.

## # History of Machine Learning

By its very definition pure machine learning is simply just curves fitting with the goal of determining the best possible curve that describes the dataset.

This is a very underwhelming task even for a machine and thus over time we have come up with innumerable methods, each one claiming superiority over the previous one until we came up with neural networks (NN), a machine learning method that has brought a revolutionary change in fields ranging from computer vision to drug discovery.

Neural NN is best described as a graph of independent mathematical units called neurons wherein each unit is responsible for taking input from the units from the previous layer and then feeding output to the next layer. The output is calculated using a simple equation:

Which brings us to the central problem of machine learning, that is the optimization of loss with respect to the parameters.

Warning

Question: What is loss function?

Answer: It is a one-dimensional function whose input is the output of the model and its output is the accuracy of the model's output.

Running this optimization on the subset of the colab notebook image classification dataset and observing the loss profile in Tensorboard shows us the power of NN and how it reaches the global minimum, unlike the other ML algorithms by minimizing loss.

Explore the jupyter notebook behind the following figure.

→

Note that any one parameter is influenced by the parameters directly connected to the units in the immediate layer. These units in turn are connected to units in the next layer in deep down subsequently overlapping multiple parameters. Until finally, it connects at the end where ultimately each parameter could be said to be linked together in some form. This linked and interdependent behavior of parameters is the key idea to the success of NN at optimizing the loss function.

The training process of NN is called deep learning which is based on another pioneering idea, that is backpropagation.

Backpropagation works by taking the gradient of the loss with respect to the neural network. As a neural network is simply a composition of functions, this gradient is computed using a simple chain rule of differentiation.

For multi-layered deep neural networks, this gradient is propagated from each layer to the previous layer recursively.

Now every machine learning method is nothing but a search through the program space and each program is evaluated on the basis of mean accuracy on a held-out dataset. So what happens when these programs become Turing complete by becoming recursive? The future of machine learning is program learning or programming learning (PL), that is training the internal representation of program (rather than the parameters) on the dataset.

But right now the major problem of PL is that it is slow and unsolvable as gradient descent cannot work due to programs being recursive and hence not differentiable.

### # Future of Machine Learning

For mitigating the latter problem, PL uses non-gradient-based methods which are really really slow.

Three methods are provided in their integral JAX package namely JIT, AutoDiff, and GPU.

Warning

What is JIT and GPU and AutoDiff in JAX?

A: JIT stands for just-in-time compilation and is a concept seen in various other languages like Java, that helps to reduce execution time at runtime. AutoDiff or automatic differentiation is the ability to compute gradients if your function for which we are computing gradients is smooth. We take advantage of the GPU (Graphical Processing Unit) by running most of the compute
