[77/200] Running: Explain the role of SynapseAI in Gaudi software stack....

🧠 LLM Response:


- Software based on the Gaudi architecture relies on the SynapseAI^{®} Stack, which manages the overall data movement through the chip. SynapseAI Software is composed of a few main layers:
    - SynapseAI Framework-sent KernelSections (FS-Framework Sections), Framework-sent and Auto-Generated Kernels (Gaudi layers), PyGaurdless, and Framework-Independent Kernels
    - Kernels that do most of the computation (i.e., matrix multiplication) are generated automatically by SynapseAI based on problem size and some user parameters. The users can also choose to write custom kernels and use a custom infrastructure to execute those kernels.
- SynapseAI enables low-level API access, presenting a broad interface of available operations for Gaudi processors. It runs on top of Habana's software agents, which create an application cluster based on a group of Habana processors. SynapseAI sits on top of lower-level libraries, such as kernel cooperative-threads for threading and tensor math.
- On CPU/GPU, PyTorch and TensorFlow have similar functions:
    - Utilize compiler and CUDA C/C++ code to accelerate math computations from "matrix-multiply" type functions
    - Create and access data structures
    - Interactive sessions for debugging
    - Kernel-only method invocations
    - Communication for data transfers and synchronization
- These functionalities allow for the creation of math operations (such as conv2d), distribution kernels, creation of data structures such as tensors, and more. PyTorch and TensorFlow are together referred to as frameworks.
- The SynapseAI software stack provides a Core API and Kernel API for application developers to write and debug their model implementations directly on the Gaudi AI Processor.
- The SynapseAI stack enables the following:
    - Data Structure access - Creation of tensors and smart buffers
    - Low-level math operations - Math computations, matrix multiplications, etc.
    - Connectivity - Ability to integrate standard libraries such as Numpy or DL frameworks
    - CPU control - Allows controlling the CPUs running on a Gaudi chip.

Where does the compiled SynapseAI code run?

All the code that is generated for the SynapseAI stack for the Gaudi processor runs on the two CPUs in a Gaudi AI processor. However, the compiled hardware kernels run on the RISC cores that control the matrix operation on the Gaudi. The SDK documentation contains information about the compilation hierarchy between CPU code and hardware-based RISC Processor systems.

What are the AI Libraries, how do I write an application with the SynapseAI stack?

Implementing an application with SynapseAI-based AI libraries starts with creating a model definition with the PyTorch or TensorFlow. Then, implement a SynapseAI model implementation by loading and analyzing the trained weights to generate Hardware Kernel Library core. After the HW Kernels library core has been built, you can call the compile and execute functions of the Core API to generate the targets for the Gaudi processors.

Are there any built-in data structures that the SynapseAI Python API has access to?

Yes, the SynapseAI Python API, which includes a wrapper for JIT, provides tensor creation and manipulation functions. These are provided through both a Tensor interface and a Smart Buffer Interface, which are described further in other documentation for that API.

How do I deploy my multi-programmed model implementation on the Gaudi processor?

- Multi-programming allows for distribution of different model parts or components across the system. Users can implement different types of model architectures simultaneously using this functionality. SynapseAI Core API could be used when writing multi-programmed applications. See the wiki documentation for further information.
- Parallel processing across multiple Gaudi processors is important for scaling performance. Scalability can be either distributed or duplicated, depending on the implementation required. These two methods of scalability are described in the documentation for the Habana SDK.

Can I actively monitor the performance of my trained model implementation on the Gaudi processor?

Yes, SynapseAI includes a performance tracking utility that allows the end user to enable profiling. Access to this requires use of the ZeroMQ Messaging Library API which is described in other documentation for that API.

Is the SynapseAI Core API only available for execution on the Gaudi?
