Here is a quick answer to those questions.

GPU tensors are implemented with NumPy to help with consuming inputs for deep learning training, predicting, and evaluating. HPU tensors are a new subclass of GPU tensors that helps to improve the execution in Python and the development of Python extensions in C, C++.

If you want to get the most out of GPU and HPU tensors, you’re in the right place.

Keep reading, and you’ll discover how to work with tensors efficiently.

## Difference between HPU and GPU tensors

The best way to think of HPU tensors is as an extension of NumPy, a specialized case for deep learning.

While GPU tensors are the fundamental building blocks of how GPU-accelerated computing is happpening.

To further understand the difference between the two, let’s have a closer look at details.

### What are GPU tensors?

GPUs are the ones that bring short and steady memory to all deep learning: CUDA^{®}, DirectML, or OpenCL™.

In general, tensors consist of an arrangement of components indexed by vectors, matrices, and so on.

You have three types of tensors: Val, Var, and Placeholder.

Var will introduce new inputs, while Placeholder enables repeated use of the `feed dict` mechanism.

### What are HPU tensors?

HPU or High-Performance Unit tensors are optimized to run on the Intel Habana hardware.

