- Conversion rates and other sales metrics
- Likes from the other members of the platform
- Front page placement
- Notifications in user inboxes
- Unique email addresses
- Foursquare shout outs
- Quantity or Frequency of social media mentions

Everyone pays attention. Everyone. Some of us have shorter attention spans than others and most of us have short attention spans when we are first introduced to new concepts.  People also do not share their attention homogeneously, they are continually giving and disregarding to target something – some targets are social, other targets are purely image based.

There are a couple of common math misconceptions.  The first is that humans all pay attention at a uniform rate.  The second is that humans hold position uniformly.

Thirdly, there is no such thing as an engaging post which in turn grabs the attention of the user.  Well I take that back, I do think there are “hooks” to getting users to lick their screens, but for the majority of people online this is not the norm and the only reason they pay attention two is for the attempt to gain something for nothing – as it is called in some circles.

1. There will be a “virus” – There’s no getting around it, but thankfully there are ways to build a successful and fruitful protocol without getting microbiologists infected in your ecosytem.
2. People will often forget about you: Your attention graph will grow in the ideal case, then as individual are reduced to drawing a wider network and the work it requires in order to engage another person they will rebuy.  This discounting issue lends itself to a significant value judgement.  Typically, at 30% discount rate as an example, your best memory will be worth 3 points in 10 years – even if a user pays attention to your post indefinitely, they will be worth somewhere like 0.xx < 0.99 in a dull infinite amount of time – ie. you need them to keep your attention over a prolonged period of time to be a “deep believer” in your thought or set of ideas.  You can only increase that back growth by giving them more money for their influence.  These individual in your network might not be like you, but you do want them to be persuaded by your posts, and you do want them to be attentive to your brand of information.

The attention graph is the key to  monetizing.  This is a much better way than simply selling your data.
