a) Cost: Machine Learning requires inﬁnite compute and memory resources which is expensive. Now a new device has to balance between performance and cost. This has made companies think about developing new devices which have Machine Learning (ML) capabilities for the cloud and edge devices such as Alexa, Google Mini.

So, we need to get the slice of new small devices where we can develop ML algorithms like CNN to support small devices. This balance between cost and performance has allowed us to develop and maintain ML-capable devices and still become dramatically popular.

b) Privacy: ML technology is driven on data, data privacy has become the biggest concern of the user. Most of the big companies like Apple, Google, Facebook have used ML models to get the benefit of their data by understanding the pattern through ML algos and make decisions based on this. This has caused unethical use of user data.

To combat this more ML algos have been shifted to edge devices to maintain privacy. So we need to build AI chipsets capable of running ML algos on edge devices.

c) Power: Traditional computer architecture is facing a power challenge to run lightweight, low power, and high performance which can bridge the gap in the data transmission resulting in intelligent behavior of the machine and improving the speed of the machine so a jet engine failure can be recognized and fixed before it causes any damage.

To solve this MIT has developed a chip called Eyeriss having 64 processing elements of around 10mm2. It can be fabricated in a few years for these requirements.

d) Performance: For binarization and quantization, 8-bit ALU is being used as 16 bit is not supported as it’s expensive. Data Center accelerators are helping achieve the perfect balance between all factors like performance, cost, and privacy.

e) Memory: DRAM is used in the present world for storage, which consumes larger power, so chipsets like Google’s TPU have been developed to enable low memory power.

Resistance Quantum Memory (RQM) could be an alternative in the future in a wide range usage where they are 50x to 100x better at saving power than current memory chips.

f) Security: To cyberattacks in the present world, physical measure limits the tampering of ML hardware. More advanced chips based on neuro-inspired hardware need to come in the market.

g) Embedded ML: Embedding ML algos like Deep Neural Networks into edge devices such as drones or self-driving cars without compromising on speed and allow real-time decisions.

h) Data: In present world everything is moving to the cloud and edge devices. To harness naturally generated data (like phone snaps or audio feedback through voice assistants) which is not usable for ML systems due to the large number of features to be analyzed needs to be analyzed. We need to train ML system with lot of data but still filtering correct data is a challenge for natural data.

i) Boundaries: Marking boundries between the devices ML capabilities whether to have minimal or maximal can be a critical decision to be kept between cloud and edge devices. Either entire data is processed on the cloud and sent to edge devices or real-time decisions based on data are made on edge devices. Eg Autonomous cars will use edge ML algos while Alexa speakers ML algos will run on the cloud.
