[86/200] Running: How does temperature affect language model output?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How does it behave at different scales?

The temperature term $\tau$ offers a way to alter the sampling distribution of a generative model. In NLG, it's used to tune fluency and variety, where elevated temperatures increase the diversity of output at the expense of fluency. At the extreme, as temperatures approach infinity, it has a fuzzy typer effect where it writes seemingly context-free gibberish.

To learn how this term affects generation, I made a Colab notebook experimenting with different temperature values and observing the impact on perplexity, diversity, and fluency. I enabled TPU in the notebook settings for better sample generation speed. The model is GPT-2 medium (345M params, FLOPS). I used token-level sampling with a fixed seed (`torch.manual_seed(0)`) to make comparisons. Here's the perplexity vs temperature plot when temperature gets high:

Perplexity vs temperature heatmap
(fixed temperature, fixed seed)

We see an expected rise in perplexity around `1.0`, the usual temperature. The model is also more bold at `0.8 - 0.9` temperature, here showing a greater tendency to produce sexual content, an effect I explored in a previous post.

For both the X and Y axis, I random sample 100 distinct prompts: from the test split of WikiText-103 and from random continuations of the WikiText-103 data set, to study complexity. In the case of "Complex" prompts, perplexity slowly rises with temperature as expected, showing the effect of the temperature.

One interesting difference is the perplexity vs temperature profile, which changes linearly around 1.0 and then gradually slopes back down to some limit.

To understand what's going on, I generated some continuations from

> "The Brookings Institution said the administration should reject free trade deals other than Nafta."

Due to space constraints, I can only show excerpts from a few continuations.

