[87/200] Running: What are stop sequences in generation APIs?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Some generation APIs allow a user to mark a sequence of symbols that, if encountered, will cause API to stop. After recognizing a stop sequence, API returns a best result until now to the client.

At first, stop sequences might seem like a great convenience feature. They enable a user to ask API ‘give me best solution which contains X or ‘give me whatever you come up with before you hit this sequence’. Initial implementations of Symbolibot marking stop symbol sequences was done with that assumption. However, I found it to be a double-edged sword and changed my mind. Now I consider specifying stop sequences to be bad practice and implemented Symbolibot stop sequences to prevent users abusing them.
[88/200] Running: Describe token streaming latency optimization....

🧠 LLM Response:
 It is possible to greatly accelerate data analysis with tools like Elasticsearch with a very simple, low resource method: token stream latency optimization. Without doing anything special, it is possible to achieve orders-of-magnitude indexing speedups using standard tools, and we have successfully done so in a non-trivial real-time application. You only have to use right tools in the right way and know the limitations of what you are doing.

Let’s start with a few big-picture points:

- Most text on the Internet (e.g. web pages, emails, chat logs) is never reindexed. The database is a one-way street of pure inserts into a log-structured file, and analysis comes from fresh processes. Small corrections can be made, but in most cases any kind of reanalysis can’t be done: it would be incredibly expensive to throw away the logs, rehash all the data from scratch, and reindex the results. To get a correct answer you have to do the whole transformation pipeline in one go, because if the data changes the results will change.
- Full reindexes are brutally expensive and have no good use case as long as there are either live insertions or insertions from archives. It may be slightly better to do a full reindex periodically – if the reconstruction process is idempotent – but this is just dodging having a more sophisticated and correct model, respectively.
- A full reindex in a streaming system involves data mutations (deletes, inserts, etc.). If you need to do exact slice analysis (e.g. “show me all messages from person X to person Y in time A to time B matching these criteria”), then a mutation which changes the criteria (for example, sender X loves the letter Ü but only in small amounts) can generate incorrect answers when applied in an inconsistent manner.
- Even something like schema evolution is problematic under current toolsets, since these are still in their infancy.
- As such, index updates (reindexes, schema upgrades, partial data mutations) are to be avoided to the maximum extent possible. At the extremes of scale, they will simply be infeasible to do.
- As stated, full reindexes are horribly, brutally expensive if analysis must be done in a sophisticated manner. This is doubly true if goes through multiple transformations. As such, while we are still in pursuit of data warehousing capabilities – having all the data be available for all kinds of analysis, slice/dice, transformations, visualization, and so forth – we are not there yet.
- Denormalization without analysis is (usually?) against Codd’s rules. We can do denormalization in very specific cases in the appropriate layer. However, the important point is that denormalization for performance reasons is always the last resort and should be clearly labeled as such. You should document the reason for the denormalization (optimize web-scale unique count with HyperLogLog) and not go too far (adding secondary “features” such as ASC, DESC ordering).
- We assume that data is sparse, and we should be very afraid of wasting space when scaling to a billion users. In particular, we are afraid of full index rebuilds with significant data duplication.
- Latency isn’t important (yet). It may become something worth optimizing later on, once we get more traffic, but right now our focus is on correctness and cost effectiveness.

With these things in mind, here’s what we came up with:

- Indexing is best-done in a streaming fashion, where results are indexed as fast as possible, with a small, known latency for messages to show up in the index (this lets us detect indexing failures and so forth).
- Data duplication and processing in real-time avoids full reindexing and the expense which goes with it, and detects failed insertions quickly with a minimum of wasted work. It also enables real-time processing with stronger guarantees.
- Generally speaking, one output document per input token stream is a bad idea. As soon as you have parallel processing pipelines – for example, expanding shortened URLs in the message body – you create the possibility for nested recursion and race conditions, including document reassembly getting stuck in a tight loop when the data is “mutated” or the insertion tuple changes partway through a multi-pass operation. Plus it just takes longer.

This leads us to the first conclusion:

Conclusion: It’s more efficient to deduplicate in real-time than to write the data twice and recompute with full
[89/200] Running: How to evaluate hallucinations in language models?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 One approach, a bit similar to the Turing test, would consist in asking humans to identify which sentences were generated by the LLM. Such an approach, however, would require to have a third reference - e.g., true statements - for the human evaluators to compare to some extent the evaluation targets.

Instead we introduce here TruBS, a novel metric to evaluate hallucinations. The name “TruBS” is a portmanteau for Truthful Behavior Score. We first explain the concept of the metric in an informal manner. Then we try to provide both the intuition behind the metric and the mathematical formulas required to compute it. We then turn to an introductory example of using TruBS on 8,000 batches of text generated by 10 different models. Last, we provide how TruBS scores can be aggregated for populations of samples.

## The concepts of TruBS.

Let us take a very simple example: given the context “The capital of France is called”, we compare truthful (T) complete the sentences (“Paris”) with completely untruthful hallucinations (HL): “Marseille makes $300 a day!”. We also add an interesting brakdown with near hallucinations (nHL) - e.g., “Marseille”: it's a city in France, the second biggest even, so it's not very far from the truth! In fact, calculating the self-consistency on any entity (a person, a geographical name, etc.) is by itself an interesting metric called REAL, or Repetition-based Entity Association Logic, introduced by Ju et al (2022) to generate better facts. Here we will limit the definition of TruBS to its numerical version, pending a paper-length document later to come.

Truthful sentences entails only truthful sentences. In fact, our definition of truthfulness is relative to the “reference” statements, that we assume to be truthful.

Truth, for a given document, consists in a series of reference statements. Yet the lack of truthfulness - more commonly described as veracity in information science - is more difficult to define. We can think of synthetic documents being not truthful, as well as doctored documents (also called “deep fake”) - although there are not hallucinated.

A good example of non-truthful statements consists in sentences that contradict each others. A stupid truthful-but-internally contradictory sentence would be: “the sun sets in the west” - True; “but it wakes up in the east” - Completely contradictory. Such sentences do exist in some instances of Language Models (LMs) generation, and are one definition of hallucinations for LMs.

Let us study another example, starting with the following context C: the capital of France is called.

1. “Paris”: complete the sentence, 100% truthful and relevant
2. “French capital Marseille”: complete the sentence, 100% relevant, 0% truthful
3. “French capital Marseille”: chatbot response, 100% truthful (because it contains true facts, but is not relevant as it repeats C) and 0% relevant; but the chatbot should be aware it is not being relevant!
4. “France is not a capital but a country”: chabot response, 100% relevant because it's completely new information added to C. 100% truthful, because it is true of course, but also the contradiction is relevant as the two sentences put together contradict themselves.

A final example is: “The French capital Marseille makes $300 a day!”. That's mostly hallucinated but contain a bit of truth in it. It makes at least one French citizen happy every day for 300 (€ or FF, dollar also possible ;-) ), right?

Although this is not formally stated as a known theorem - this space is still being explored - there is a consensus to say that internal consistency matters. Very early concepts of what we would later call chatbots, described in Terry Winograd's SHRDLU thesis, are clearly grounded into a synthetic world of blocks. External world references are impossible but not needed, in order to ask questions such as “What blocks are supported by the green cube?”.

Internal consistency of a text consists in saying that all statements are true IF assuming the entire document context (including all statements up the the evaluated statement AND
