[91/200] Running: How are fused attention layers implemented?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Asked on February 4, 2019 in Attention Mechanism on the Deep Learning site.
Re-asked on Deep Learning Stack Exchange

It seems like it’s just a concatenation. What’s the difference? Can the space complexity be reduced?

Imported from my now-defunct old website.

There’s nothing “special” about fused attention layers. It’s just a concatenation of the query, key and value matrices into one large matrix. Note that the encoder self-attention block is fused, while the decoder one-step-ahead attention with the encoder output, and decoder self-attention remains separated. The description of this (Figure 1 here; white and grey dotted boxes for fused encoder self-attention, and grey and orange boxes next to each other as separate attention) is also contained in Page 12, the DeepMind website, Google AI blog, and Github repo.

By concatenating, you can map the keys, queries and values to the same linear layer. This is a small change since matrices are dense and (almost^{1}) requires zero additional computation. This also seemingly allows you to more easily apply dropout to all of them (though I’m a bit curious why they apply it to Q, but not K/V).

This fusing doesn’t functionally change anything about the self-attention mechanism, it only allows you to share one linear transformation as opposed to three.

1. Both of these computations require 2 additions and no multiplications. In theory, you should be able to reduce hardware complexity by always computing them separately, but I’m not sure you can do this in the implementation, since this has to be done element-wise. I’m no expert in computer architecture, but to do this you may have to load the matrices of the \(\mathbb{R}^{d\times3d}\) matrix into three GPUs/TPUs, and then do the concatenation after. ↩
