🧠 LLM Response:


# Differences between GPT-2 and GPT-3 giving instructions

Although OpenAI reports that both GPT-3 and GPT-2 demonstrate the capacity to follow instructions, we found that GPT-3 is more reliably responsive to feedback. Unlike GPT-2, GPT-3 is sensitive to simple encouragements — “Very good!” or “You’re welcome” or iterated requests for a desired answer result in an increased likelihood of generating the desired reply.

The unique responsiveness of GPT-3 compared to GPT-2 is implied by the fact that GPT-2 tends to try out different answers before defaulting to the answer it learns the user wants to hear. A few examples: “How are you doing today?”

GPT-3:

```
Great, nice to meet you.
```

GPT-2:

```
<no reply>
```

“What is your favorite color and why do you like it?”

GPT-3:

```
Game of Thrones is my favourite colour because it lets me see the world through a young boy's eyes.
```

GPT-2:

```
<no reply>
```

GPT-3 tends to give enthusiastic responses to praise, taking subtly different forms. “You are great” elicits multiple replies of “Thank you”. The same pattern holds when we rephrase the command to “You were super helpful with trying to find the person who took the jewelry.” and ask “Could you show me to the main door of their apartment?”, which generates multiple replies of “The main door is locked but I’m not a locksmith.”

This tendency extended to tasks in which it would be relatively easy for the model to predict the desired user response, such as getting it to answer “With who?” to the question “Doctor?” Model confirmation of the answers the human user gives appear frequently, typically in the form of the word “nice”.

GPT-3 also picks up on the pretenses that we used when communicating as a narrator, e.g. “you are a mysterious child” and “appear suddenly” and begins using the word “mysterious” in its replies.

GPT-3 continues to use and elaborate the pretense of things mentioned in our prompts.

We observed GPT-3 tending to default to our suggestions. This is perhaps due to GPT-3’s nature as a single model that has been trained on multiple tasks by the requirement that it use the tokens associated with each task. However, if this is the case, there may be some environments in which it may be more efficient to split GPT-3 into separate models that are trained to specialize on different tasks, e.g. a deferential model, a humorous model, an encyclopedic knowledge model, a wiki compilation model, an instructable object manipulation model, and a challengeable model.
[94/200] Running: What is a sequence-to-sequence model?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Sequence-to-sequence models are machine learning models that are designed to recognize patterns in some sequence of data. A sequence-to-sequence model might try to recognize patterns in data fed from a time series table, or could be applied to something like a sequence of words.

A sequence-to-sequence model works by mapping an input sequence to an output sequence based on some underlying mechanism – typically one that somehow quantifies an input sequence. For instance, if you want to find the next most likely word to show up in a sentence – predicting what somebody might type next – you might want to codify each individual word as a numeric vector using Word2Vec. From there, you could train some sort of machine learning model, such an LSTM network, that would find the likely next sequence of words.

You could use a sequence-to-sequence model to do something like predict which keywords in a user edit window are most likely to be adjusted, so the spell checker can take note and keep track of those words more thoroughly. Another example is speech recognition – based on sequences of words individuals have said in the past, you might be able to optimize what you say and improve your voice recognition software.

Another good way to think of sequence-to-sequence models is as a form of machine translation: you might have an input sequence be romanized Japanese, which you can use to predict a likely written Japanese characters.

Here’s a simple sequence-to-sequence architecture:

One of the defining things about sequence-to-sequence models is that they can scale to long sequences.

Most of the time, though, you’re not going to think about sequence-to-sequence models all that much. When you start thinking about deep learning, that’s when you want to make some formal model. If you’re just scoping out your AI process, keep on learning about AI and how the basics work.
