[114/200] Running: Explain cosine similarity in embeddings....

🧠 LLM Response:


## Simply Expressed

Cosine similarity, in the context of embeddings, refers to the similarity between two vectors when cosine is used as a geometric measure. In this way, similar embeddings have a high degree of semantic similarity. It should be noted that cosine similarity is related to distance defined between vectors and, though very useful, is by no means irrefutably the measure of similarity.

## Explained Deeply

Embeddings represent some input, often discrete in nature, as a vector in a continuous space. The vectors themselves have two primary properties: direction and length or, more accurately, length squared. Recall that the square root of the sum of squares forms the length.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\Vert x \Vert = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{2}}\end{eqnarray}\]

Square root in this expression carries $ O(N^{2}) $ computational complexity, which is enough to turn many embedded algorithms into, at worst, prohibitive. In practice, the square root is often omitted from distance calculations, all without affecting discrimination, and this is known as using the L2 distance, as opposed to calculating the L2 norm.

\[\begin{eqnarray}d_{L_{2}}^{2} = \sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{2}\end{eqnarray}\]

When the dot product is used as the similarity measure, other factors must be considered than that of distance alone. The dot product is defined as the sum of the pairwise product of $x$ and $y$ terms.^{1}

\[\begin{eqnarray}d_{dot} = \sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}y_{i}\end{eqnarray}\]

Although at first glance this might seem to indicate a measurement of similarity, it does not, because the way that these latent factors are normalized means that a high value could indicate either similarity or opposition. This is readily illustrated. Assume two vectors, termed $x$ in red and $y$ in purple, having common (right) and opposite (left) attributes.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{x} = [1,1, \ldots ], \, \vec{y} = \left\{\begin{array}{c}[1, 1, \ldots]\\[10pt] [-1, -1, \ldots]\end{array}\right.\end{eqnarray}\]

The dot product fails to discriminate between these vectors, as illustrated by their respective sums.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{x} \cdot \vec{y} =\left\{\begin{array}{c}\sum_{i=1}^{N}1 \times 1 \\[10pt] \sum_{i=1}^{N}-1 \times 1\end{array}\right.\end{eqnarray}\]

Outer observation suggests that the dot product is, in fact, measuring opposition, indicating that the squared negative dot product must be used.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{x} \cdot\vec{y}^{2} =\left\{\begin{array}{c}\sum_{i=1}^{N}1 \times 1 \times 1 \\[10pt] \sum_{i=1}^{N}1 \times-1 \times 1\end{array}\right.\end{eqnarray}\]

One way to implement assessment of cosine distance is to scale the distance by the product of the individual vector lengths or, more accurately, the squared lengths, which still achieves discrimination without the $NP$ time computational overhead. Notice that by doing this, the negative squared values automatically become positive similarities.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{x} \cdot \vec{y} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}y_{i}}{\Vert x \Vert \Vert y \Vert}\end{eqnarray}\]

Another way to calculate cosine distance is to first normalize the vectors, the scaling factor-squared then being removed in both the numerator and denominator. This is known as applying the L2 normalization, the taxonomy inherited thereby.

\[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{x}_{L_{2}} = \frac{1}{\Vert x \Vert}\vec{x}\end{eqnarray}\] \[\begin{eqnarray}\vec{y}_{L_{2}} = \frac{1}{\Vert y \Vert}\vec{y}\end{eqnarray}\] \[\begin{eqnarray}\
