[116/200] Running: How do shared embeddings help in decoder-only models?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Thu Nov 17 2022

At the heart of current large language models is GPT3. GPT3 is an auto-regressive (causal) language model built out of transformer decoder blocks. Since it is a decoder-only model, it uses a causal mask to ensure that during both training and inference, the decoder has access only to the tokens from before the location in the current sequence.

But what makes GPT3 so good at zero-shot learning? The main advantage for this comes from the improved pre-training task. GPT3 models utilize the same task as GPT2 to predict the next token. And if we look at the model theory behind a discrimative (classification problem), the major benefit comes from the fact that it performs many classification tasks, similar to the training objective, that allows it to learn good representations. But during pre-training in an auto regressive language model, we are using the same task that is during inference. So basically we are pre-training with the same task as that of inference. And GPT3 capitalizes on this, from the huge amount of compute it uses, and the way it trains which allows it to have a really good set of representations that it learn from.

In both these eras, we are using the weight sharing method, where the same weights are being used in the decoder and encoder. This is the main idea being used in the original transformer paper, where the authors have leveraged the fact that since the architecture of both encoder and decoder are similar, their input and output can be learned by the same set of representations, and leverage the same weights of the encoder output to initialize the decoder input. This kind of weight sharing allows the decoder to have the same input embeddings as that of the encoder.

This kind of method also reduces the params needed – the encoder and decoder vocabulary both share the same input and output space, and so the dimensions are the same which avoids creating redundant params.

1. https://www.philschmid.de/quora-answers-how-are-the-embedding-matrices-shared-between-the-encoder-decoder-in-the-transformer
