🧠 LLM Response:

Hi, I am Krishna. Writing about machine learning and deep learning. And here to help you with any doubts in machine learning and deep learning. To understand how the forward and backward pass are different in the transformers let us recall how feedforward neural network (FNN) works. An example of a FNN is the ReLU activation.

`def forward(x) = ReLU(x) def backward(dL/dy) = 1 when x<=0, 0 when x>=0`

In the above, `x` is the member transiting from the previous layer. The output of FNN is `y` .

The gradient `dj/do` is calculated wrt the optimization function `j`. The dy/du will have the information about the layer after current layer that is responsible for the optimization function. The dj/do will pass the gradient with respect to the output.

\[ \frac{dj}{do} \]
\[ \frac{dy}{do} \frac{dj}{dy} \]
\[ 1 * \frac{dj}{dy} \]

Above, `y = o` to simpify the math. In the backward pass the gradient flow takes a path opposite to the path taken in the forward pass.

At the beginning of the backward pass, the transformers fill their memory with keys and values.

`z = concatenate (All values at all timestamps)`

The concatenation here is applied on the sequence length. In the backward pass the feature dimension is untouched. The concatenation is applied on the key dimension in the multi head attention block.

Figure: Multihead Attention block

The dot product self-attention block deals with the following variables.

The

\[Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK^T)V \]

The softmax should be calculated using the following formula. Let us assume the head is corresponding to one time stamp.

`layer[:, t, :].softmax(dim = -1)`

note: `:` represents all elements

#### The derivative of softmax

\[ \frac{d}{dz_i} softmax(z) = softmax(z)(1_{i} - softmax(z))\]

The above derivative is derived from the following.

\[ \frac{d}{dz_i} softmax(z) \]
\[ \frac{d}{dz_i} e^{z_i} / sum_{j=1}^{K} e^{z_j}\]
\[ (1_{i} - e^{z_j}/(sum_j e^{z_j})^2)e^{z_i}\]
\[softmax(z)(1_i-softmax(z))\]

Derivative of the dot-product attention

\[ \frac{d}{dQ} QK^TV = KV^T\]

Q after derivative is becomes keys weighted by the values transposed.

```
`The derivative of dot-product attention with respect to K or V for a fixed Q` \( \frac{dZ}{dK} = Q V^T \) \[ \frac{dZ}{dV} = softmax(QK^T)^T Q \] `We can derive the above given` \( QK^T V=Z \) `by applying the derivative of matrix multiplication, sum of extermes, etc.`
```

The above example is for only one head.

