[121/200] Running: What is speculative decoding and how does it help?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Speculative decoding is one of a lot of techniques that Intel processors provides for optimizing program's execution speed. It mostly happens in processor's fetch stage. The following thread from videohelp.com has some basic introduction on speculative decoding: Speculative Hardware Decoding, what is this?

Let's dig into it a little. Supporting by advanced analysis on the code block, Intel processor are able to identify several probable instructions paths, even if they are not real. Instead of waiting the previous instructions finish, the processor will decode/execute those instructions in parallel with previous logic instructions. If the real control flow is included in those probable paths normally the speculative decoding is proved to be correct, then the benefits are pretty large on overall decoding speed. Otherwise, the speculative decoding may contribute nothing but increasing the power consumption during the decoding.

Most subtitle Blu-ray/DVD rippers on Windows can work around with subtitle stream by extracting the subtitles and burn them to the video in advance. Obviously, this approach is able to bypass the problem in concern, because the player would directly show the bitmaps of subtitles that being burned over the video, instead of retrieving and decoding the run-length-coded subpictures from the original video. But this approach still can't pass Dolby's DRM protection, yet.

Based on the currently available information, there are some ways that Dolby MIGHT lock the subtitle display.

- ## Storing the subpictures in encrypted container.

Actually, subpicures are not supposed to be encrypted in the container, no matter for Dolby TrueHD or DTS-HD Master Audio. After ripping the Blu-ray or copying DVD Main Movie to intermediate container format, just extract the subpicture stream from the intermediate file and convert them to soft subtitle, then the rippers could insert those subtitles into the ripped video directly.
- ## Incorporating DRM protection into MPEG-4 Part 30 spec.

RealVideo 4 was proved to incorporate DRM protection in their MP4 file format, in a way that looks like MPEG-2 TS protected by DTCP. Maybe Dolby and DTS did the same thing on their container format or their subpicture stream spec? Although it is very unlikely.
- ## Subpicture format is different from spec.

This could happen. But it's also very unlikely. If subpicture format is different from the spec, both PowerDVD and TMT3/5 would refuse to recognize and decode the subpicture data. There would be no subtitles displayed in playback.
