Recent work has explored inference serving at scale for LLMs. Providing latency and throughput guarantees is difficult due to the variability of real-world serving workloads where requests are often nonuniform. This is an unsolved challenge. Commercial solutions to the problem include many knobs that we do not fully understand, and academic research is only beginning to explore the topic.

These challenges, along with the much greater interest in new models 4, warrant a careful exploration of serving performance characteristics. In this work, we study serving challenges in the context of community models, with a focus on frontend popularity variation and limited hardware resources.

We find that the two primary challenges are as follows.

(1) Too many LLM requests:

This is the most commonly faced challenge. The reason is the capacity of a single GPU to compute an LLM inference is quite small. Thus, handling very high RPS load, serval GPU devices are required. Of course, adding more devices can dramatically improve RPS but not the latency. For example, in the Hugging Face post, More GPUs, Less Latency, with limited GPU resources, a single model server can serve the 8x cost of using 8x servers with the resources required to handle 1,500 RPS on GPU.

