1x : 1502.24 (requests per second)
2x : 10534.05
4x : 21058.57
8x : 37970.49
```

Why? To improve RPS, the effect of multi-processes is greater than that of multi-device. Many requests must go to the same model server with a single process. Thus, the server/process needs to handle very big distribution. This dramatically increases the latency for the local generation of tokens relying on the exact results of the first token generation. Even if our computer has 1 GPU, 8 or more devices may not guarantee high RPS and low latency because of memory.

(2) Too many tokens:

This challenge is generated by the memory of the GPU device, mainly because many LLMs are not designed for generation efficiency in both RPS and long generation texts. Thus, we need to design our LLMs, which should satisfy their cost-effectiveness and many applications. We will explore this topic in the future.

We demonstrate that profiling is required to make decisions when serving on commercial or research hardware. In particular, through our results, in a dynamic workload of unbounded size, (1) pure alignment with increase-then-max throughput policy is not good, and (2) using helper threads to accelerate inference serving results is very important. Possible implementations: batching techniques and LLM-specific acceleration such as Flash and follow-up work.
