[126/200] Running: What are logits and how are they interpreted?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How do colours relate to logit scale in 3-vial posteriors?

See this post on 3-vial for colour scale, below I provide more detail on logits and how they are interpreted.

The PCT uses three categories to categorise decisions about whether or not the genetic material on an item is transferred by contact in a crime:

1. 3-vial (a “portfolio” of decisions) – A vial was used to collect the non-local crime item that shows at least one interpretable peak from the stain, the vial was profiled for DNA and the mixed profile has been interpreted. Three hypotheses are considered about whether or not the non-local item (i.e. the crime item, which will give one of the PCTvials) is the source of DNA on the other (previously local) item. The results are given as the combination of the transfer rate (TR), the persistence rate (PR) and the recovery rate (RR).
2. Negative control – A negative control was tested and no human DNA was detected. The profile that is recovered represents environmental background for three hypotheses that relate the negative control to environmental DNA matches.
3. Background profiling – No control was used (no negative control and no three-vial PCT) and there is only a single profile retained from the non-local item in a questioned/control pair, that may contain environment background that would not be present in the control.

### The 3-vial PCT framework

For the 3-vial PCT framework the logit is defined as follows,

where

The \(p\)-values in the equation are LRs, for example, \(p_H\) is the likelihood ratio for testing the prosecution hypothesis \(H\) versus the general alternative \(\overline{H}\), against the data, where this hypothesis may be for example,

- a transfer hypothesis (with a transfer rate TR)
- a persistence hypothesis (with persistence rate PR)
- an intermediary (another person) hypothesis with a recovery rate (RR)
- environmental background hypothesis (with no positive control to compare to)

Note that \(p\)-values are still called likelihood ratios due to the frequency (i.e. \(p_H=\Pr(\text{data}\mid H)/\Pr(\text{data}\mid \overline{H})\)). But of course, thinking about them as \(p\)-values makes most sense as e.g. a \(p_h\)-value is the probability of the data under hypothesis \(H\), and we are used to thinking in terms of \(p\)-values, and we know for example, that \(p<0.05\) is strong support for the hypothesis considered, the fact that this is also the value of the likelihood ratio is less prominent in our thinking, whereas the ordering of likelihood ratios is, e.g. “larger likelihood ratio equals more evidence for”.

### Interpretation of the logit

The logit score (or logit for short) can be expressed in terms of three ingredients: \(\log_2 LR_{t/p},\log LR_{t/i}\) and \(\log LR_{t/u}\) \begin{equation} \begin{split} \text{logit} &= \frac{\log_{10}p_H-\log_{10}p_{\overline{H}}}{2\log_{10}2} + \frac{\log_{10}p_{\overline{HTB}}-\log_{10}p_{\overline{H}}}{2\log_{10}2} + \frac{\log_{10}p_{\overline{H}\overline{T}}-\log_{10}p_{\overline{H}}}{2\log_{10}2} \\ &= \frac{\frac{\log_2 p_H}{\log_210} – \frac{\log_2 p_{\overline{H}}}{\log_210} }{2} + \frac{\frac{\log_2p_{\overline{HTB}}}{\log_210}-\frac{\log_2 p_{\overline{H}}}{\log_210}}{2} + \frac{\frac{\log_2p_{\overline{H}\overline{T}}}{\log_210}-\frac{\log_2 p_{\overline{H}}}{\log_210}}{2} \\ &= \frac{\frac{\log_2 p_H}{\log_210}}{\frac{2}{\log_210}} –
[127/200] Running: What is log probability in token prediction?...

🧠 LLM Response:


If the output token is rare in the training dataset, the model will predict a low probability of around `0.1%` (with a log probability of `-6.91`). Conversely, if the output token is very common, the model will predict a high probability of around `80%` (with a log probability of `-0.22`).

Probabilities values always stay between 0 and 1. Such values are usually very large in token probability analysis, therefore, log probabilities are calculated and used instead.

Token probabilities are not yet converting into log probabilities in the dashboard. To convert probabilities to log probabilities, you must start by using the following formula:

$$log(p) = ln (p) / ln(2)$$

And simplify it, as follows:

$$log(p) = 1.44 lnp$$

For example, a probability outcome of 0.1% converts to a log probability value of `-6.91`. Until this feature is added to the dashboard, you should use the previous formula to convert the probabilities into log probabilites.

