[128/200] Running: How to use `TextStreamer` for streaming output?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The best kept secret in the C++ Documentation of Qt: Extending the QTextStream Class. So after more time spend researching than implementing the I came across an undocumented and not mentioned in the Qt-5 C++ book feature `QTextStream::QTextStream(&QIODevice)`.

The signature in itself is not that special but it makes all the difference to provide a reference in addition to the conventional `QTextStream::QTextStream(QIODevice*)`. The reference based constructor does not acquire ownership (hence you can safely use the stream parameter in the constructor with a this pointer).

My algorithm requiring this feature is simple and a special case of conditional text transformation. I want to implement infinite undo (and redo) with compressed stack history based on the XDiff algorithm. Soon however I realised that the original string is constructed first and streamed afterwards once it is finished.

This is not desirable as it can mean a lot of RAM and GC is used (heap fragmentation and decrease of performance). In an interactive application the text fetching method is not ideal either as text is obtained in chunks (aka scanlines starting at certain coordinates). Especially when the text is initially streamed (upon loading) this is a source for latency as it is streamed to RAM just to be discarded again right afterwards.

On modern desktops Qt seems to implement the streams directly on top of the backing files (not confirmed, could also be caching) and disk speed limits computing speed. As you know streaming is pretty much a standard in modern web development but streaming is everywhere and has been for a while. Working with it in C++ (especially visualisation of large data-sets) feels more natural and performant even though premature optimization is evil.

Hardly any resources (QString is surprisingly convenient) for I/O strings to primitive data-types conversion without constructing only to stream require some experience. So to my surprise the undocumented QIODevice reference signature provides the means to simplify applying an object as a transformation filter which is a powerful paradigm and promotes inversion of control flow.

So my eventual data structure is private to my formatter and does not even need the stream to be given but store and retrieve it in response to public load and save slots (provided for `QTextCursor` text selection only). QPlainTextEdit provides enough operations on top) already, and the stream format can be plugged into the document as shown below (Strings for all string conversions):

```
class TextStream : public QObject {
    Q_OBJECT
    QIODevice& stream;
    QString str;
public slots:
    void load(const QTextCursor&, const QTextCodec*) {
        stream.setDevice(&str);
        stream.setCodec(codec);
        str.clear();
        stream << fmt.load(reader.toPlainText());
    }
    void save(const QTextCursor&, const QTextCodec*) {
        stream.setDevice(&str);
        stream.setCodec(codec)
        str.clear();
        str << Qt::endl << fmt.save(reader.toPlainText());
    }
};

class PlainTextEdit : public QPlainTextEdit {
    connect(&textStream,
            SIGNAL(load(QTextCursor*, QTextCodec*)),
            SLOT(setTextCursor(QTextCursor*)));
    connect(&textStream,
            SIGNAL(save(QTextCursor*, QTextCodec*)),
            SLOT(appendPlainText(QString*)));
};
```

I/O is allocated on the stack for single use (RVO). The stream itself is provided by `QFile` which is owned by the plugin interface which is owned by QMainWindow and its ApplicationWindow subclass. The `TextStreamer` itself is tested standalone and is exported by the formatter.

The `TextStreamer` can safely be used for reentrance as QTextStream inherits from QIODevice which defines buffered seek operations. Incremental loading and saving is not used in this case and can be implemented by QTimer::interval which is a number of milliseconds where 100 simply means 1 second.

