14 aug 2017

At SeQura we have a lot of different customers of differing sizes. For us it is very important that each retailer receives a high-quality service and quickly but we also need to treat all our customers with the same respect no matter their size.

When we built our first version of our product at SeQura, we made the decision to store a lot of the information in a relational and normalized database, MongoDB. This seemed like a good idea at the time, we used many-to-many relationships, flattened the data models, and indexed properly. As more and more customers signed up to use our widgets and our services, we saw that many queries against this data store were becoming very slow. This was due to either read-contention issues or simply poor query design. Now, at this time MongoDB has maxed out the memory capacity for our largest AWS machine and we had complicated shard keys set up on things like `purchases`.

Let's take a look at an example `mongo` collection that we use. This is a document from a subdocument we store in the `Retailers` collections called `receipts_by_i18n`:

```
{
  en: [{
    id: '4lwpu0vxUZrdo'
    background_color: '#00FF11',
    goodwill_logo_url: '',
    main_text: 'Welcome to our shop',
    sub_text: "Our shop's name is Mr. Joes & snacks",
    logo_url: '/assets/resources/header-logo.jpg',
    background_header_url: '/assets/resources/header-bg.jpg',
  }, ...
}
```

As you can see there are a lot of fields (we stripped several out for brevity) but this is for each language of possible languages that has been configured at runtime. To fetch the information needed, we have to do a client-side query on `Retailer.receipts_by_i18n.EN/ES/CAT/??`. As you can imagine, we need to optimize all of these bad performance queries.

Preferably, we should rearrange our models in order to fix this but we will need several poll requests and performance optimization already, so the first thing we consider is a sharding the `Retailer` collection. We need to get away from multiple reads on one object but a sharded collection might not give us what we need.

The new `Retailer` model looks like this:

```
{
  in: [{
    id: '4lwpu0vxUZrdo'
    background_color: '#00FF11',
    goodwill_logo_url: '',
    main_text: 'Welcome to our shop',
    sub_text: "Our shop's name is Mr. Joes & snacks",
    logo_url: '/assets/resources/header-logo.jpg',
    background_header_url: '/assets/resources/header-bg.jpg',
    retailer_id: '5b0aad5cac538400aa6047f3',
    resource_id: '4lwpu0vxUZrdo',
  }, ...
```

Everything is put into a single `sharded` collection, without indexing on `resource_id` and simplified on keys. Now we are able to use in Mongo, `[{ retailer_id: '5b0aad5cac538400aa6047f3' }]` to query ONCE agaisnt the shard servers, e.g., not all of the multiple subdocuments. This gives us a nice and fast retrieval time for this information that is tied to a specific retailer and we can now use the speed we need for the high requests we are seeing due to our growth. Again, I stress the fact that this might not be your best option at all. You should refactor first, fix later. We just knew that we were going to need to do a rewrite very soon and we didn't want to waste the time to refactor anything that is being replaced anyway.

