[150/200] Running: How does deep caching work in inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Deep caching is only used in the query cache.

The deep caching functions are used by the condition generator and query generator. Deep caching is currently used to cache the execution plans for queries and conditions as those are the only deep cacheable tree fragments. The execution plan cache entries stores the execution plan fragments generated by analyzing the fragments of individual conditions or subqueries.

When a query fragment is generated, if it can be deep cached (i.e. if it supports deep caching), the cache is searched to see if a previously generated cache entry can be found. A cache hit ignores the condition cache entry generation logic. However, if a cache miss occurs the cave entry is generated and then becomes the preferred technique to reuse execution plan pieces for that type of structure/substructure. When a cache entry is generated the coefficient (optimizer statistics) of the query tree node occupies the position under which it should be. The assumption is that only values in the inputs to the cache (i.e. the coefficient of the tree node) will cause a cache miss or a change in the cache content. The contents of a deep cave are hashed based on the properties of the branch and the input properties. Perhaps the key is based on the type of the tree fragment, name and schema, and the coefficient of the tree node.

For example, when the second child of a JOIN is being analyzed, a cache hit will trigger a reuse of all levels for the second child. Serializable mode checks indicate that if there is no serializability situation on that branch the entire branch can be reused for that node. A search of the cache is triggered if any of the following is true: analysis is capable of using deep caching, the cache is enabled, the depth is not exceeded, and the last child has not been already cached.

```

        Boolean doSearch()
        throw StandardException
        {
          ...
          // Make sure that the optimizable tree node can be cached.
          if (cacheEntryExists(nodeId, lastChildAnalyzed+1)
          {
            if (cachedNodeInfoForOtherBranchesOK(nodeId, lastChildAnalyzed + 1))
            {
              if (nodeId == theJoinNode.nodeId)
              {
                // The caching algorithm is not yet implemented for Join.
                // This situations is handled during regular processing.
              }
              else
              {
                if (isCacheSerializable(nodeId, lastChildAnalyzed+1))
                {
                  // Reuse the deep hash table tree from cache.

                  reuseDeepHashTableTree(nodeId), lastChildAnalyzed + 1);
                  return false;
                }
                else
                {
                  // First eliminate the cache and analyze all levels.
                  getScanAndSelectivityInfoAtBottom(marker.treeId);
                  maybeUpdateCostEstimates();
                  topLevelUncachedHashTableTree(lastAnalyzed);
                  return false;
                }
              }
            }
            ...
        }
```

Reusing deep caching checks that all the inputs to the cache remain unchanged and if so they remain in cache. If any of the inputs have been modified the entire cache for that node and its dependencies is wiped out. The limit on a cache pass is not applicable if the goal is reuse.
[151/200] Running: What is a prompt-truncation error?...

🧠 LLM Response:


If you get the error message "Not enough space on document" the truncation warning,  the master is too large for prompting to fit, so you need to free-up some space before you can add another new prompt. There are several ways to achieve this. 1. You could remove a prompt which isn't needed. 2. You could remove the  "Static prompt entries" from one or more of the existing prompts (to remove spacing or to space things more efficiently). In the Prompt dialog of one prompt you can remove Static prompt entries by pressing the delete key on your keyboard - in this case, the Type is "Static". This way you can remove some or all of the static entries and type them freehand in the definition. 3. Another option is to add additional pages to the master, rather than having the prompts all in one table. Before creating additional pages, first copy the Existing Prompts off of the main master sheet. (Once you expand a Master to a multi page Master, you don't actually have a first page any more, so the existing prompts page goes away.) To copy: File menu > Copy Prompts to new page To expand the Master to multiple pages: Page menu > Delete a Page. In the resulting dialog, select "Retain Prompts".  Take away the slash  Then: Page menu > Insert a Page. In the resulting dialog, make sure that the check box "Split prompts on new page" is checked. Then the existing prompts show up on the first page but are no longer being inserted so you can free up some room there.
[152/200] Running: Explain the role of tokenizer config files....

🧠 LLM Response:


readtext and many cleansing functions are based on tokenizer, which can be defined via a tokenizer config file. The file can be string name or path to YAML file or language code or language code with country.

## Intro

Text mining requires a consistent understanding of tokens and constituents. A token, for example, is typically a word, e.g. “word”. Sometimes a word is a compound, e.g. “word house” is a word, which can be found in a dictionary or not, but is nevertheless considered as “clean” word. However, for some purposes, we want to get to the “basic” words the compound is made from, i.e. “word” and “house”. We use the term constituents for that. Constituents can be of different types, here words, but it is also possible to define constituents as punctuation, letter categories, linguistic categories, etc. For this purpose, we have implemented a “meta-tokenizer” based on a tokenizer config file which allows us to define certain basic operations on tokens. We call it meta-tokenizer because readers can use different tokenizers (packages or engines) for their own purpose, but we then require users to manipulate “their” token into final tokens by use of our meta-tokenizer. Using the meta-tokenizer allows different users with different base-tokenizers to arrive at meaningful tokens for analysis in the same manner, applying a defined set of rules (cleaning steps). For using the tokenizers (e.g. the corpus package), users have to specify the functions used for tokenization, stripping, numeric and punctuation conversion separately. This is not needed for tokenizer config files, as preprocessing is done simultaneously. Tokenizer config files also enter additional information into the `documents` object (fields `wordlang`, `sentencelang`, `wordlangcode`, `sentencelangcode` with language code). Furthermore, they allow users to choose their target entities (constituents) which can be arbitrary textual expressions. They may include letters, letters plus punctuation, just punctuation, just routine information like XML tags etc.

By standard, tokenization works on a per “segment” basis. A segment is whatever a reader (`readtext`) or cleaner (e.g. `iterativetagger`) recognizes as basic text segment. They can be sentences, paragraphs, lines, text snippets or entire texts. A segment seldom is an expression that is connected to meaning (except for segments which are sentences). The meta-tokenizer then transforms these segments into tokens on which linguistic analysis can be conducted. Consider the following example:

```
library("quanteda")
# use quanteda as generic tokenizer since it allows comprehensive control
s1 <- tokenize("This is a text_00.!")
# tokenizer config file (be brave and type "broken")
s2 <- tokconfig("broken", s1)
```

`s2` now looks like this:

```
# texts with sentences as segments
texts(s2, "texts")
```

