[155/200] Running: What is inference latency and why is it important?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Inference latency is the time it takes a machine learning algorithm to recognize an object and tell you what it is in reference to other objects. A low inference latency speeds up applications such as facial recognition or voice interfaces, where it can make the difference between a marketable product or a non-viable system.

Inference latency may not sound exciting but it is a topic that will have a huge impact on the global economy over the coming decades. It is the bottleneck that is holding back deployment of artificial intelligence into many fields, such as speech recognition, facial recognition, autonomous vehicles, object detection for industrial vision systems, and, more generally, edge computing, where the brains of the system are integrated onto the chip, often because network latency is unacceptable, as when you are driving a car.

Smart home speakers are a good example of how much inference latency can affect the marketability of a product. The first models took a long time to respond, partly because their queries had be sent to the Cloud and back before the answers could be synthesized. Newer models that have the algorithms on board may be promising, but their responsiveness will depend critically upon their inference latency.

The stakes couldn’t be higher for modern industry. Given that the global economy tilts continually toward services and high value-added products, anything that has to do with information and communications technology (ICT) technology will be hugely popular. For example, online retail sales worldwide in 2016 totaled about $2 trillion, according to the US Census Bureau. That’s 1/26th of the global gross domestic product (GDP) of roughly $52 trillion. The total value of e-commerce sales online is thought to be about four times larger at $8 trillion.

Advertisers can reach people via social media worldwide. Who could have foreseen that cellular communications, invented decades ago, would enable advertising, which is the most ancient form of commerce in human history? Cellular communications, which is a 5G communications technology, and AI are already driving global markets. Edge computing in ICT and AI generally will shift the telecoms industry ever more towards data, as the 5G standard evolves to include millimeter wave. 5G operators are going to have to know a whole lot more about their customers, because they will be delivering an increasing volume and variety of services to those customers. Knowing your customer is the basis of the modern economy.

These applications that collect vast volumes of data from sensors and search for patterns and meaningful differences require AI to handle the massive data streams in real time. To process the queries and respond realistically, AI algorithms need to minimize latency.

