## Abstract

LLaMA is one of the newest open-source large language models released within the last year. They took everyone by surprise by significantly reducing the size of their models without sacrificing performance. For example, their largest model, in particular, is a 65B parameter model. This is about half of the size of the leading GPT-3 175B model.

By shedding the top-heavy layers, LLaMA models already offer advantages in terms of having a smaller memory footprint over some of the larger Transformer-based models out there, which is great to see. These powerful tools aren’t only exclusively in the hands of the giants such as Google, Meta, and OpenAI anymore. You can now run these models in your own closet (note: I’m not liable for any fire hazard here). Personally, I came across the work of Gigantum and have been using it to great success to fit my own models.

Community-built tools and knowledge are becoming better by the day thanks to the early innovation and support from the large tech companies.

What about the 65B LLaMA model, though? Is that not completely inaccessible to us? Well, the honest answer to that is… maybe, depending on your hardware of course! Most of us today don’t have machines with terabytes of memory available on the market just to run one AI model. For perspective, running (requantized) 65B parameter LLaMA model on a single GPU requires at least 196GB of GPU memory. Maybe you have a few more GPUs available but running a model like that on multiple GPUs is certainly not feasible for everyone.

Thankfully, more and more tools for model compression are becoming available as well. State-of-the-art techniques before, such as quantization, distillation, and pruning, can now be done automatically with some custom Wheatley edits to improve the results even more.

In this tutorial, I’ll show you how to compress your LLaMA models further using quantization in Wheatley alongside some other tricks to make models more compact and less memory intensive.

