[158/200] Running: What is a quantized model and how is it served?...

🧠 LLM Response:


A quantized model that has static weights can be configured to be served in FP32, FP16, INT8, INT16 or UINT8. Dynamic weights can be configured to served as FP32 or FP16. By default, a quantized model is served as Int8 on platforms that support it.

What is quantization?

In the context of machine learning and deep learning, quantization makes it possible to represent a neural network’s parameters in lower-precision data types. If the conversion to the lower-precision data types is performed on a trained neural network, this process is called post-training quantization, and it’s supported by both TensorFlow Lite and ONNX.

Post training quantization is also sometimes described as post-training integer quantization: it’s a technique that conceals the values to integer powers of 2 than their current values.

The process’s purpose is to reduce the size of the model: models that take up less memory will execute faster on some devices, or require less interactions with the device’s disk. Lowering the precision of the parameters of a model could make it more energy-efficient in some scenarios.

Before post-training quantization, the parameters of a machine learning model typically reside in floating-point data types. With post-training quantization, they typically get converted to integers.

Post training quantization can be applied to many common operations, and it’s typically a fast process. Depending on the framework, it should be possible to apply post-training quantization with a single command.

For example, the post training quantization Python package quantize_model below displays a model quantized with TensorFlow Lite.

For example, the use of eight-bit quantization can theoretically increase energy efficiency by up to a factor of four.

What is quantization-aware training?

Quantization-aware training is a process by which a quantized integer version of a model can be trained to a degree that it can be a functionally equivalent to a floating-point model.

It may be referred to as quantized symmetric quantization based on symmetric quantization. This process can be used for eight-bit or 16-bit representations. If a model is trained that’s way “off” from where it needs to be, then the symmetric lower and upper integer bounds may be different: a process called asymmetric quantization.

To learn more about quantization and quantization-aware training, including whether the trade-offs are worthwhile and why there’s a need for quantization-aware training, check out this talk from WordPress Vancouver.

What software is available for quantization?

The TensorFlow Lite framework has a module to perform post-prior quantization. Usually, a neural network is trained with full-presision floating-point numbers, and later quantized to represent those weight values with eight-bit integers.

Facebook has open-sourced the quantization-aware training extension for the PyTorch framework. When the extension is applied to run quantization-aware training, parameters will be stored with full-precision floating-point weights but training is performed using lower precision weights.

Caffe has built-in quantization support, optimizing performance and memory usage.

TensorFlow has also introduced quantization-aware training, which is supported by Keras.

The ONNNX framework, which TensorFlow, PyTorch and Caffe2 are among the contributors to, has support for making neural networks take up less memory and run faster on supported devices.

Pytorch also supports quantization.

What variety of a framework is needed for quantization-aware training?

Quantization-aware training functionality is typically provided by specialized flavors of machine learning frameworks.

Does quantization reduce the neural network’s accuracy?

Whether the quantized model will be just as accurate as the full-presicion model, or not, depends on the model architecture and how you define ‘accuracy’.

For example, quantization should not negatively affect the random

One way of determining whether the quantized version is inferior or not is to do something called ‘model equivalence’. Equivalence checks between a full-precision model and quantized model are performed by running the full-precision model and the quantized version using the same inputs: if the values or the activations are closer to the outputs of the full-precision model, it can be concluded that the quantized model is indistinguishable from the original model, or equivalent.

