[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How do they impact system performance? What approaches can we take to reduce their impact?

Let’s start by understanding what server cold start is first.

A cold start occurs when an endpoint receives a request for the first time. A number of issues can manifest themselves before the server is ready to handle that request, ranging from a momentary delay to timeouts.

Serverless models, for example, can take anywhere from 10ms up to a minute or more before being ready to respond to the first request. This is usually longer than subsequent response times. The time between the endpoint request and the time the endpoint is available is called the serving latency.

Cloud compute resources are billed per second. In the case of Extreme Low Density Memory (XLD) models, you will be paying twice per second, even if the workload takes only milliseconds to execute. To understand why, consider this:

On average, an XLD model will take about four seconds per cold start. Once the service is running, it takes just a few milliseconds to execute the task. Depending on the use of the model, you might need dozens (or more) cold starts per day, per deployment. Now, imagine that your workload actually takes about 10 milliseconds to run. Because serverless workloads are billed per second, you’ll be charged $100 every time your model loads. Of course, if that time increases to 20 or 30 seconds, your cost will increase unnecessarily.

With ML models, server cold starts are usually due to one of two things.

The first is a necessary warmup of the code and the language runtime, especially in cases when unique environment and configuration options are being used. Installing and configuring the model takes time.

The second cause is loading the model from disk to memory. This can take anywhere from a handful of milliseconds with an uncompressed 1 MB model file to as much as 10 seconds or more for larger models. 100 GB models, for example, will take much longer.

Cold starts, especially with large models, will have a major impact on your cloud compute costs. This is true regardless of whether you’re running serverless or containers.

There are a few ways to avoid server cold start costs, but in order to discuss these solutions, let’s first understand when do server cold starts occur.

In the case of our hypothetical geology application, the AI model provides insights (i.e., it predicts the rock type) based on an image of the drill core. While the crew prepares the drill core sample, the application could be sending preemptive requests to keep the model warm. But this would be completely pointless if the model did not need to be woken up.

It’s not just images. Our application example also accepts text, where users can ask for customized insights for specific purposes. As the previous user is finished up with the application, the models that are likely to be needed by the next customer can and should be loaded automatically in anticipation.

You can build your own solution for identifying and initiating requests ahead of the actual user request, but a better approach is to predict what degree your model is being used. This is what Anodot Machine Learning (AML) does. See “Why leverage Anodot ML solution?” below for more information.

For more on AML, download our whitepaper, “Anodot ML whitepaper: Best practices for AI deployment with AML.”

If you’d like to discuss how AML can help you and your team manage cold starts and reduce your cloud costs, schedule a call with one of our DevOps experts today!
