🧠 LLM Response:


In recent years, multilingual models including Flan-PaLM have shown significant achievements in natural language processing. The ability to serve these models at scale is important for both research and for people using their applications. In this post, we describe our effort in scaling multilingual models for streaming inference while working on Tracery, a voice-based real-time collaboration tool. Tracery allows its users to record meeting notes in real-time and to perform queries like “Explain x in 5 bullets” where the natural language query “x” can be in any language supported by the model, including Spanish, Hindi, Japanese, etc. When users are recording notes, multilingual models enable these queries for parts of the transcript which is not restricted to a single language. We describe three key challenges when it comes to serving multilingual requests.

1. In our experience, multilingual models can lead to significantly slower inference performance in streaming settings. Our experiments (discussed in detail below) show that Flan-PaLM inference is up to 15x slower in non-English languages. This performance slowdown makes the models harder to serve in real-time and challenges monolithic approaches to serving.
2. A sizable percentage of queries happen in multiple languages. As shown later in this post, over a quarter of the queries are in groups of 13 or more languages. These multilingual queries make model selection difficult, as selecting the most representative language is insufficient.
3. Searching for the most representative language adds additional latency to the serving pipeline since search needs to happen first.

What are the open questions?

There are several ongoing directions for serving multilingual models. These approaches can be generally categorized into two categories.

1. Language-specific models: The database can index the model for different languages separately (as was done in this paper). As these models grow in number and size, the database needs to determine what models to load for inference. This could lead to further challenges for model management.
2. Intents: This common approach involves building classifiers on the training data to map user queries to predefined tasks. Different models can then be delegated to different intents. However, classifiers may not perform well on out-of-domain datasets and, hence, may not generalize in the inference environment.
In contrast, we explore an inference-only approach. The key ideas behind our approach are to decompose the workloads dynamically and exploit a database’s inherent fault tolerance (queries can be retried) for recovering from errors when a model does not return the right response for a given query. Through this, our solution addresses all three challenges highlighted above. Because the approach does not require additional training for intent classifiers or replicating models with different language specifications, our solution scales to any number of languages, intents, and models. The solution achieves latency goals while being fully independent of training workloads and eliminating the overhead of building generic prediction systems.

What is the approach?

As shown in the figure, we follow a “best effort” approach using a loop in which some SPLOCS are sent to a model for prediction and the results are set to be committed. If the models take too long, or if the model concludes that it cannot complete the computation in 10 seconds, the results are not committed and the loop continues to the next step. This approach enables us to explore easier SLOCs in later rounds while maintaining a tight service level agreement (SLA): Of failures, 95% take no longer than 500 milliseconds.

Contrary to existing techniques on multilingual serving that look at intents, we focus on the language specificity of the input and demonstrate effective strategies to dynamically split the workloads by iteratively deciding and routing to different models. Empowered by model isolation, our system maintains low latency with only a small overhead percentage (~1% average) to launch new tasks. The system also enables the database to utilize underutilized executors by launching adaptive number of SPLIT jobs while the original pipeline still analogizes all normal aggregations from triggers. For example, with a given 30-second wait time, the original model queues might already be enough for all the 1000 requests. However, when no other job is waiting in the queue, the number of worker nodes might go up to 200.

What is the performance?

Overall, the system averages less than 6 seconds for submission and 500ms for querying, compared to over 100 seconds SLO
