The development and engineering of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning models have flourished in recent years with the rise of sophisticated digital technologies. Powerful internet giants like Google, IBM, Amazon, Facebook, and Microsoft have all launched proprietary machine learning and natural language processing models that permit humans and machines to collaborate in remarkable ways.

Large language models (LLMs) are the basis of these technologies and work by ingesting trillions of data points and signals from sources on the public internet such as Wikipedia, social media platforms, digital textbooks, and blogs. LLMs also take in structured data from companies that collect data for profit (e.g., acquiring personal data and reselling it to data brokers) and nonprofit organizations that don’t directly profit from the data they collect like universities, charities, and government entities. LLMs analyze these tendencies in the data and extract patterns that instruct the algorithms that ultimately dictate the outputs of a particular AI technology.

However, pre-training LLMs on the information that lies on the open internet without proper bias and toxicity checks can propagate misinformation on controversial topics like abortion, gun control, the Ukrainian invasion, or election peddling. It also raises issues of proper consent to use personal data if the data provider is unaware of the aggregation their data is a part of, its intended use, and/or how it impacts branding decisions.

AI technologies and their respective LLMs have become increasingly rich in the insights they generate. They can be leveraged for inventive purposes in subjects such as climate science, medicine, and technology. But with great power comes great responsibility, and tech companies should follow certain guidelines to prevent their AI technology from reinforcing false narratives or abusing user data.

### Benefits of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Models

AI technologies provide valuable social benefits by streamlining familiar processes and providing nearly instantaneous results.

For example, ChatGPT, Chatbot, and Bibo have revolutionized customer service capabilities for many businesses by quickly answering user questions and complaints through a streamlined automated process centered around natural language, voice-assisted commands, and prompts used to fine-tune the AI. These AI technologies also reduce operating costs and prioritize time-sensitive responses for customers.

OpenAI and IBM describe additional benefits of their AI technology by pointing to their LLMs’ ability to critically analyze news content to measure the veracity of their claims, predict political and financial futures given selected datasets, and even code.

However, these advances rely on LLMs and their training at a deep level of granularity, so LLM engineers must sift through a considerable amount of data signals to fine-tune their model, a task that can be challenging and daunting. However, if LLMs aren’t properly pre-trained with certain data inputs, the AI they help animate can promote misinformation and harm user privacy.

### Disparities in AI/ML Ethics

There are four major categories of the way that LLMs, corpus resources, and algorithms shape the assumptions and outcomes of an AI: data bias, data privacy, cross-cultural generalization, and ethical concerns.

##### Data bias

Since natural language data (i.e., the dataset written in layman’s terms that makes up the corpus) is sourced from English-centric webpages and social media posts, AI and machine learning models run the risk of unconsciously perpetuating biases from their users, incentivizing further misinformation accumulation in online communities, and overgeneralizing their outputs.

For example, if an AI such as a search engine trains a model that misconstrues fashion terms as racial slurs in their predictions, then a user searching on the topic may seek out information that validates the misleading model assumption.

Additionally, data bias and conflicting private interests add friction to cross-cultural generalization. Often the data an LLM is trained on was generated from a singular country or set of countries, mainly the United States. However, because many AI technologies are now global, the data must shift and include other sources at a granular level. Each culture will have a different relationship to the AI technology (more on that below), but because data will now be gathered from personas, the LLM will experience more precise signal detection on the topics and service required from a community or individual.

##### Data privacy

