## Tutorial Overview

This tutorial is divided into three parts; they are:

Layers as Functions
Block of Layers
Build a Convolutional Neural Network Model

## Layers as Functions

Machine learning neural network models can be described as a sequence of layers where each layer performs a pattern of computation on an input to the layer.

Accordingly, layers can be described as functions that perform a series of operations over a data structure, such as a Tensor or list, then return the data structure.

An artificial neural network is an example of a model organized as a series of layers, starting with an input layer that receives the raw training data and finishing with an output layer that provides the data to make predictions.

Sometimes a model might organize layers into blocks that repeat a computation, often ending with a summary layer that bridges to the next block.

An example is a dense block used with Multilayer Perceptron models. It might involve a Dense layer that transforms the features into a new representation, followed by the application of a nonlinear activation function.

A Residual Block (also called ‘residual network’) might involve the application of a series of 1D, 2D or 3D Convolution layers, followed by the application of a normalization and then an activation function.

The inputs, as well as final outputs of a block, are tensors that can be organized in a n-dimensional dimensional grids such as rows and columns, width and height, or depth, width, and height.

Shaping the dimensions of the data through the blocks can, therefore, be framed as a function that maps from one set of dimensions to another. This is the basis of learning to reconfigure (or transform) input into output data.

The Keras library provides an interface called the functional API that can be used to design neural network models as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of layers.

This API can be a good fit for this function-view of designing blocks of layers that can be combined to create a network of layers into complete models.

