## Block of Layers

The standard convolutional neural network architecture comprises of a series of repeating blocks of layers.

A block begins with a set of convolutional layers, one or more dense (fully connected) layers, and a softmax layer for classification.

The blocks are stacked sequentially into a network, where the filter map outputs from one block become the input to the next block.

This facilitates building deeper networks, where the network may be constructed from stacked blocks of convolutional layers and pooling layers.

Repetitive blocks can simplify the model architecture. It is efficient and requires to define and connect only the blocks together.

You can define a block-layer that transforms conv pooling pairs into dense layers.

The easiest way to achieve this is by using the Keras functional API which allows you to treat model layers as you would any other functions.

You need to import the function you are required for Keras API. Import input, concatenate, multiply, average, Conv2D, and Dense from functional models of Keras.

